WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:05.340 --> 00:00:07.582
We'll finish up this
last additional topic.

00:00:07.582 --> 00:00:08.790
It's not an additional topic.

00:00:08.790 --> 00:00:09.910
You've already
seen a bunch of it,

00:00:09.910 --> 00:00:11.368
but I thought it
would be fun to go

00:00:11.368 --> 00:00:12.870
into a little bit more detail.

00:00:12.870 --> 00:00:14.920
And at the end of
today's lecture,

00:00:14.920 --> 00:00:17.370
we'll talk about
just summarizing

00:00:17.370 --> 00:00:22.780
the whole class and what do you
do now, where to go from here.

00:00:22.780 --> 00:00:24.000
So, OK.

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:28.150
So jumping back into this.

00:00:28.150 --> 00:00:30.720
So we were talking about
cardinality constraint

00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:34.590
but the cardinality could be
applied for example to the,

00:00:34.590 --> 00:00:36.840
let's say, a first
difference in which case

00:00:36.840 --> 00:00:38.970
you would be doing
piecewise constant fitting

00:00:38.970 --> 00:00:40.470
and that's obvious.

00:00:40.470 --> 00:00:45.030
You take a first
difference matrix here.

00:00:45.030 --> 00:00:47.010
So you take a first
difference matrix D,

00:00:47.010 --> 00:00:52.330
and then if Dx has
a small cardinality,

00:00:52.330 --> 00:00:56.160
it means you have a small
number of jumps in x.

00:00:56.160 --> 00:01:00.540
And you could just do this
for the second difference

00:01:00.540 --> 00:01:03.150
and things like that.

00:01:03.150 --> 00:01:07.480
If you do second
difference, then you

00:01:07.480 --> 00:01:09.160
get a second difference matrix.

00:01:09.160 --> 00:01:12.490
And if this matrix
multiplied by x

00:01:12.490 --> 00:01:15.340
is sparse or has
cardinality of less than k,

00:01:15.340 --> 00:01:19.210
then this is exactly when
x is piecewise linear,

00:01:19.210 --> 00:01:21.640
or I guess that's the way
people would normally say it.

00:01:21.640 --> 00:01:24.250
I might say piecewise
affine with something

00:01:24.250 --> 00:01:29.800
like k kinks in the change
of slope and stuff like that.

00:01:29.800 --> 00:01:35.200
So we can handle all these kinds
of constraints in this convex

00:01:35.200 --> 00:01:37.210
cardinality framework.

00:01:37.210 --> 00:01:37.720
OK.

00:01:37.720 --> 00:01:39.790
So now we get to the heuristic.

00:01:39.790 --> 00:01:41.020
You know it already.

00:01:41.020 --> 00:01:41.770
You've seen it.

00:01:41.770 --> 00:01:42.940
It's really dumb.

00:01:42.940 --> 00:01:46.510
It's wherever you see the
cardinality replace it

00:01:46.510 --> 00:01:51.610
with a multiple of the
one norm or just add

00:01:51.610 --> 00:01:56.530
some l1 regularization
to the objective.

00:01:56.530 --> 00:02:00.490
And then gamma
here is a parameter

00:02:00.490 --> 00:02:03.670
that you use to achieve
the desired sparsity.

00:02:03.670 --> 00:02:05.800
So that that's the heuristic.

00:02:05.800 --> 00:02:07.450
You've seen this a lot.

00:02:07.450 --> 00:02:08.838
There are some other versions.

00:02:08.838 --> 00:02:09.880
You're going to see them.

00:02:09.880 --> 00:02:12.820
They're actually better.

00:02:12.820 --> 00:02:14.470
That's a weighted l1 norm.

00:02:14.470 --> 00:02:16.960
And this one I've
heard people call

00:02:16.960 --> 00:02:18.730
that an asymmetric l1 norm.

00:02:18.730 --> 00:02:22.510
But I hate that because it's not
a norm if it's not symmetric.

00:02:22.510 --> 00:02:26.980
In other words, you actually
would penalize things

00:02:26.980 --> 00:02:30.640
being small or less
than 0, bigger than 0

00:02:30.640 --> 00:02:32.020
with different slopes.

00:02:32.020 --> 00:02:33.610
By the way, we're
going to see later

00:02:33.610 --> 00:02:35.200
exactly why you might do that.

00:02:37.780 --> 00:02:40.900
But generally speaking, it's
just the l1 norm heuristic.

00:02:40.900 --> 00:02:44.030
That's used in a lot of fields.

00:02:44.030 --> 00:02:46.930
So here's the minimum
cardinality problem.

00:02:46.930 --> 00:02:48.620
And you start with this problem.

00:02:48.620 --> 00:02:49.120
It's hard.

00:02:49.120 --> 00:02:52.030
And you replace it with
this one, which is convex.

00:02:52.030 --> 00:02:53.890
And so it's easy to solve.

00:02:53.890 --> 00:02:55.930
Now, of course, that's
just a heuristic.

00:02:55.930 --> 00:02:57.830
When you solve this problem.

00:02:57.830 --> 00:03:00.140
You have not solved
that problem.

00:03:00.140 --> 00:03:01.720
This is merely a heuristic.

00:03:01.720 --> 00:03:07.590
A quite good one, in fact,
for solving the top problem.

00:03:07.590 --> 00:03:09.510
OK.

00:03:09.510 --> 00:03:12.240
If you had cardinality
constraint problem,

00:03:12.240 --> 00:03:14.290
lots of ways to do that.

00:03:14.290 --> 00:03:16.860
One way is to literally just
replace this with an l1 norm

00:03:16.860 --> 00:03:19.590
and you have something
that looks like this,

00:03:19.590 --> 00:03:22.020
or you can make it
an l1 regularization.

00:03:22.020 --> 00:03:24.030
You can actually add
this regularization term

00:03:24.030 --> 00:03:25.830
to the objective.

00:03:25.830 --> 00:03:28.200
And then you adjust
beta and gamma

00:03:28.200 --> 00:03:32.070
until the cardinality
is what you want.

00:03:32.070 --> 00:03:38.280
So these are the most basic
ways that people might do this.

00:03:38.280 --> 00:03:43.800
Something called polishing is
actually super interesting.

00:03:43.800 --> 00:03:44.970
That's this.

00:03:44.970 --> 00:03:47.730
What you do is you
use an l1 heuristic

00:03:47.730 --> 00:03:53.220
to guess merely the
sparsity pattern of x if x

00:03:53.220 --> 00:03:57.090
is the thing you want sparse.

00:03:57.090 --> 00:03:59.940
So you run an l1
heuristic, adjust gamma

00:03:59.940 --> 00:04:02.790
until you think they're
supposed to be 15 nonzeros

00:04:02.790 --> 00:04:04.900
or you feel like
that's right for you,

00:04:04.900 --> 00:04:06.850
then you get 15 nonzeros.

00:04:06.850 --> 00:04:08.650
And what you simply
do is you record

00:04:08.650 --> 00:04:13.300
the locations or the indexes
of the non-zero entries.

00:04:13.300 --> 00:04:14.620
So this is phase one.

00:04:14.620 --> 00:04:18.220
Then you go back and
you resolve the problem

00:04:18.220 --> 00:04:20.470
with only those
as the variables.

00:04:20.470 --> 00:04:23.530
All the others are now 0.

00:04:23.530 --> 00:04:26.530
And you remove any
regularization if you had that.

00:04:26.530 --> 00:04:28.090
So that's called polishing.

00:04:28.090 --> 00:04:32.200
So this would be very,
very commonly done.

00:04:32.200 --> 00:04:33.680
Actually, it's interesting.

00:04:33.680 --> 00:04:37.390
So if you ask two
different people

00:04:37.390 --> 00:04:39.310
who do statistics is
this a good idea when

00:04:39.310 --> 00:04:40.600
you're doing model fitting?

00:04:40.600 --> 00:04:44.050
And most of my friends
will say, no, no, no.

00:04:44.050 --> 00:04:45.880
You should never do
this in statistics

00:04:45.880 --> 00:04:48.940
because the whole point was
not only did you sparse,

00:04:48.940 --> 00:04:51.250
did you do feature selection
but you shrunk also

00:04:51.250 --> 00:04:53.317
the coefficients.

00:04:53.317 --> 00:04:55.150
And then other people
I know they would say,

00:04:55.150 --> 00:04:56.360
no, we always do this.

00:04:56.360 --> 00:04:59.427
So I'm not quite sure.

00:04:59.427 --> 00:05:01.010
I think it depends
on the application.

00:05:01.010 --> 00:05:03.520
So an example of where
this is always done

00:05:03.520 --> 00:05:09.760
is when people they
have gene response data.

00:05:09.760 --> 00:05:13.590
So you have 20,000 gene
expression features

00:05:13.590 --> 00:05:15.420
and you're trying to
predict something.

00:05:15.420 --> 00:05:19.680
So first step is you do l1
regularized logistic regression

00:05:19.680 --> 00:05:22.830
you find the 22 genes
that you believe

00:05:22.830 --> 00:05:24.750
will give you a
good logistic model.

00:05:24.750 --> 00:05:26.280
Again, if you know
what I'm talking

00:05:26.280 --> 00:05:28.680
about that's fine, if you
don't, that's also probably OK.

00:05:28.680 --> 00:05:31.557
So you get these 22, you locate
those 22, then you go back

00:05:31.557 --> 00:05:33.390
and you just fit a
logistic regression model

00:05:33.390 --> 00:05:35.160
with just those 22 genes.

00:05:35.160 --> 00:05:36.330
Everybody got that?

00:05:36.330 --> 00:05:36.900
Yeah.

00:05:36.900 --> 00:05:39.410
Is the sparsity
pattern dependent

00:05:39.410 --> 00:05:41.180
on the initialization?

00:05:41.180 --> 00:05:43.280
Would you get a different
sparsity pattern?

00:05:43.280 --> 00:05:44.660
Absolutely not.

00:05:44.660 --> 00:05:45.590
No.

00:05:45.590 --> 00:05:46.280
No, no.

00:05:46.280 --> 00:05:49.220
So there's no such thing as
initialization, number one.

00:05:49.220 --> 00:05:52.250
And number two, if
it turns out when

00:05:52.250 --> 00:05:55.700
you solve that problem there's
not a unique solution, which

00:05:55.700 --> 00:05:58.245
is entirely possible,
then it's possible

00:05:58.245 --> 00:05:59.870
that you could have
different solutions

00:05:59.870 --> 00:06:05.120
but they'll all have
the same optimal value.

00:06:05.120 --> 00:06:09.702
But no, that's not an issue.

00:06:09.702 --> 00:06:11.660
If that's an issue we're
in deep, deep trouble.

00:06:11.660 --> 00:06:13.910
Because the general
story for convex

00:06:13.910 --> 00:06:15.620
optimization goes like this.

00:06:15.620 --> 00:06:16.580
I've done this.

00:06:16.580 --> 00:06:19.040
I've gone off and give
talks about something

00:06:19.040 --> 00:06:20.960
and I'll say that's
a convex problem.

00:06:20.960 --> 00:06:23.082
And then they'll just
say, we can solve it.

00:06:23.082 --> 00:06:25.040
And then people will say,
how did you solve it?

00:06:25.040 --> 00:06:26.670
Did you use a
conjugate gradient?

00:06:26.670 --> 00:06:27.530
Did you use this?

00:06:27.530 --> 00:06:28.685
Interpoint method?

00:06:28.685 --> 00:06:32.975
I'd actually literally just
say, it's none of your business.

00:06:32.975 --> 00:06:34.688
It's very good at
irritating people.

00:06:34.688 --> 00:06:37.230
And then finally they'd say,
but you must have used a method.

00:06:37.230 --> 00:06:38.220
I said, "Oh, I used a method.

00:06:38.220 --> 00:06:38.820
I did.

00:06:38.820 --> 00:06:39.390
You're right.

00:06:39.390 --> 00:06:39.990
I did.

00:06:39.990 --> 00:06:42.282
But I'm not saying what it
is because it's not relevant

00:06:42.282 --> 00:06:43.950
because it's a convex problem."

00:06:43.950 --> 00:06:46.650
So what that means
is basically--

00:06:46.650 --> 00:06:49.740
I told them finally I can think
of 50 methods all of which

00:06:49.740 --> 00:06:53.290
would work, all of them.

00:06:53.290 --> 00:06:55.410
It's like if somebody
does least squares

00:06:55.410 --> 00:06:56.633
and you go, how'd you do it?

00:06:56.633 --> 00:06:57.300
How'd you do it?

00:06:57.300 --> 00:06:59.400
Do you use QR
factorization, SVD?

00:06:59.400 --> 00:07:01.050
Conjugate gradient, LSQR?

00:07:01.050 --> 00:07:04.230
And the answer is unless
the problem is ginormous

00:07:04.230 --> 00:07:06.240
and you're under extreme
real-time constraints

00:07:06.240 --> 00:07:07.950
or something like that,
it's no one's business.

00:07:07.950 --> 00:07:09.533
They would always
get the same answer.

00:07:09.533 --> 00:07:10.350
So that's why.

00:07:10.350 --> 00:07:13.360
And certainly, it has nothing to
do with the initial condition.

00:07:13.360 --> 00:07:17.720
So that's just my
reaction to that.

00:07:17.720 --> 00:07:18.220
OK.

00:07:18.220 --> 00:07:20.770
So this is polishing.

00:07:20.770 --> 00:07:21.910
It's interesting.

00:07:21.910 --> 00:07:25.720
And it depends on
the application,

00:07:25.720 --> 00:07:27.530
whether or not you
should do polishing.

00:07:27.530 --> 00:07:29.530
I think in a lot of
statistical fitting methods,

00:07:29.530 --> 00:07:33.610
you should not because the
entire point was to regularize.

00:07:33.610 --> 00:07:34.610
Well, it was two things.

00:07:34.610 --> 00:07:37.660
It was to do feature
selection and to regularize.

00:07:37.660 --> 00:07:39.280
So you shouldn't do this.

00:07:39.280 --> 00:07:41.740
Many other things like if
you're doing electronic design

00:07:41.740 --> 00:07:43.870
or mechanical
engineering or something

00:07:43.870 --> 00:07:46.570
and you want a sparse
thing because you

00:07:46.570 --> 00:07:50.080
don't intend to build a space
frame with a million bars

00:07:50.080 --> 00:07:53.260
but in fact, only
236, then you do

00:07:53.260 --> 00:07:58.570
do this because once you know
which ones are actual bars then

00:07:58.570 --> 00:08:02.020
you go back and
solve the problem.

00:08:02.020 --> 00:08:02.995
That make sense?

00:08:06.320 --> 00:08:08.560
So now we're going
to give a couple of--

00:08:08.560 --> 00:08:12.730
so we gave a couple
of interpretations

00:08:12.730 --> 00:08:15.070
as to why this l1
heuristic would work.

00:08:15.070 --> 00:08:18.220
And one that is not
bad is this idea

00:08:18.220 --> 00:08:21.550
that compared to
a square penalty,

00:08:21.550 --> 00:08:23.830
an absolute value
penalty keeps up

00:08:23.830 --> 00:08:27.250
the pressure to make the thing
small all the way up until you

00:08:27.250 --> 00:08:28.900
get to 0.

00:08:28.900 --> 00:08:31.750
And by pressure, I
mean, the derivative.

00:08:31.750 --> 00:08:33.789
It's either plus or minus 1.

00:08:33.789 --> 00:08:35.740
Plus 1 if you're negative.

00:08:35.740 --> 00:08:38.887
Whereas for a square
penalty, once you get small,

00:08:38.887 --> 00:08:41.470
it doesn't care anymore because
the penalty is basically small

00:08:41.470 --> 00:08:42.502
squared.

00:08:42.502 --> 00:08:43.960
That's actually,
believe it or not,

00:08:43.960 --> 00:08:48.500
a perfectly good explanation
for why an l1 norm.

00:08:48.500 --> 00:08:52.960
And for that matter, any
penalty with a kink at 0

00:08:52.960 --> 00:08:55.570
is going to have this property.

00:08:55.570 --> 00:08:57.820
So, in fact, there's a
penalty that's very funny.

00:08:57.820 --> 00:09:00.770
Everyone knows about
the Huber penalty,

00:09:00.770 --> 00:09:03.430
which is it's a
Frankenstein penalty that's

00:09:03.430 --> 00:09:07.100
quadratic and then
transitions to linear.

00:09:07.100 --> 00:09:12.660
So there's another one
which is l1 for small things

00:09:12.660 --> 00:09:15.340
and then transitions
to quadratic.

00:09:15.340 --> 00:09:17.170
Everybody picture that?

00:09:17.170 --> 00:09:18.340
And you're going to get--

00:09:18.340 --> 00:09:21.530
that's going to be a sparsifying
regularizer for example

00:09:21.530 --> 00:09:22.960
if you use it as regularizer.

00:09:22.960 --> 00:09:25.040
And do you know
what it's called?

00:09:25.040 --> 00:09:26.890
This is kind of cutesy
but I don't care.

00:09:26.890 --> 00:09:29.500
That's called the BerHu penalty.

00:09:29.500 --> 00:09:30.400
Sorry.

00:09:30.400 --> 00:09:33.130
It's Huber-like trans--
anyway, it's stupid.

00:09:33.130 --> 00:09:34.120
Who cares anyway?

00:09:34.120 --> 00:09:36.550
But the point is that's an
example of a sparsifying

00:09:36.550 --> 00:09:38.290
penalty.

00:09:38.290 --> 00:09:39.470
OK.

00:09:39.470 --> 00:09:39.970
All right.

00:09:39.970 --> 00:09:42.558
So we gave that as
an example and I

00:09:42.558 --> 00:09:44.350
think you've actually
already done problems

00:09:44.350 --> 00:09:48.190
where you have experienced
this sparsification.

00:09:48.190 --> 00:09:50.530
But now we're going to
give a couple of other very

00:09:50.530 --> 00:09:52.120
cool interpretations.

00:09:52.120 --> 00:09:56.110
So one is let's
solve this problem.

00:09:56.110 --> 00:09:59.230
Let's attempt to solve
the cardinality of x,

00:09:59.230 --> 00:10:00.940
minimize this.

00:10:00.940 --> 00:10:03.097
Subject x is in a
convex set and then

00:10:03.097 --> 00:10:05.680
all of the x's are between plus
and minus-- all the components

00:10:05.680 --> 00:10:10.160
of x are between plus n minus
R. So that's this thing here.

00:10:10.160 --> 00:10:10.660
OK.

00:10:10.660 --> 00:10:12.077
So what we're going
to do is we're

00:10:12.077 --> 00:10:13.202
going to write it this way.

00:10:13.202 --> 00:10:14.890
We can introduce
some new variables zi

00:10:14.890 --> 00:10:16.223
and they're going to be Boolean.

00:10:16.223 --> 00:10:17.770
They're going to
be either 0 or 1.

00:10:17.770 --> 00:10:20.800
And we're going to write the
constraint that xi is less

00:10:20.800 --> 00:10:22.330
than R is this way.

00:10:22.330 --> 00:10:25.870
And what this means is if
zi is 0, it means xi is 0.

00:10:25.870 --> 00:10:29.350
If zi is 1, we express
the idea that it's less

00:10:29.350 --> 00:10:33.730
than R. So this
problem is completely

00:10:33.730 --> 00:10:35.260
equivalent to that one.

00:10:35.260 --> 00:10:36.717
So they're identical.

00:10:36.717 --> 00:10:37.300
Not identical.

00:10:37.300 --> 00:10:38.590
Sorry, equivalent.

00:10:38.590 --> 00:10:42.310
Of course, they're not
identical or equivalent.

00:10:42.310 --> 00:10:46.450
This one is no easier
to solve than this.

00:10:46.450 --> 00:10:50.140
Actually, this one you could
pop in to what are called mixed

00:10:50.140 --> 00:10:51.560
integer linear programming.

00:10:51.560 --> 00:10:54.160
So a MILP solver.

00:10:54.160 --> 00:10:57.610
And if you're lucky, you might
get a solution or something

00:10:57.610 --> 00:10:59.260
like that, who knows?

00:10:59.260 --> 00:11:02.770
If you're unlucky you might be
waiting an hour or something.

00:11:02.770 --> 00:11:03.340
OK.

00:11:03.340 --> 00:11:07.490
So what we're going to do
is we're going to relax.

00:11:07.490 --> 00:11:08.878
That makes perfect sense.

00:11:08.878 --> 00:11:10.420
What we're going to
do is we're going

00:11:10.420 --> 00:11:12.415
to take these z's which
are Boolean variables.

00:11:12.415 --> 00:11:13.510
So it's 0 or 1.

00:11:13.510 --> 00:11:16.550
We're going to relax them to
this weird quantum mechanical

00:11:16.550 --> 00:11:18.740
state that can be
anywhere between 0 and 1.

00:11:18.740 --> 00:11:21.300
So that's a relaxation.

00:11:21.300 --> 00:11:26.060
Now that gives you this
problem here, this top problem.

00:11:26.060 --> 00:11:28.790
And let's see, let's
take a look at it.

00:11:28.790 --> 00:11:30.230
Oh, good news is that's convex.

00:11:30.230 --> 00:11:31.790
So that we can solve.

00:11:31.790 --> 00:11:33.410
That's completely tractable.

00:11:33.410 --> 00:11:35.580
But if you stare
at it carefully,

00:11:35.580 --> 00:11:40.010
you'll realize that if
I divided things out,

00:11:40.010 --> 00:11:43.940
this is actually the
epigraph, the sum of z's here

00:11:43.940 --> 00:11:45.470
is the epigraph--

00:11:45.470 --> 00:11:47.360
this is the epigraph
representation

00:11:47.360 --> 00:11:53.780
of the l1 norm of x divided
by R, or just take R equals 1.

00:11:53.780 --> 00:11:58.940
So this problem, which
is literally it's

00:11:58.940 --> 00:12:03.010
the relaxation of the original
Boolean problem just simply

00:12:03.010 --> 00:12:04.660
becomes our l1 heuristic.

00:12:04.660 --> 00:12:08.740
By the way, this l1
norm here thing was--

00:12:08.740 --> 00:12:11.680
sorry the l infinity constraint
was critical to do this.

00:12:11.680 --> 00:12:15.700
Without that, the
relaxation is nothing.

00:12:15.700 --> 00:12:17.440
It gives you no
information at all.

00:12:20.645 --> 00:12:22.270
This also suggests
all sorts of things.

00:12:22.270 --> 00:12:26.440
If your variables have
different ranges or bounds,

00:12:26.440 --> 00:12:30.820
then you probably shouldn't
be doing an l1 heuristic,

00:12:30.820 --> 00:12:32.860
you should probably
be a weighted l1 norm,

00:12:32.860 --> 00:12:37.020
weighted by the inverse of the
bounds or something like that.

00:12:37.020 --> 00:12:38.340
OK.

00:12:38.340 --> 00:12:41.280
Now when you solve this
problem, these are the same.

00:12:41.280 --> 00:12:43.740
They get the same optimal value.

00:12:43.740 --> 00:12:45.960
You get a lower bound
on the optimal value.

00:12:45.960 --> 00:12:48.660
It's not usually a
particularly good one.

00:12:48.660 --> 00:12:51.820
So this makes sense.

00:12:51.820 --> 00:12:54.660
Now, you can also interpret
this in a really cool way

00:12:54.660 --> 00:12:58.170
as a convex envelope,
which I will explain.

00:12:58.170 --> 00:13:00.420
So if I have any
function at all,

00:13:00.420 --> 00:13:02.880
here's a function non-convex.

00:13:02.880 --> 00:13:04.980
Then it's convex envelope.

00:13:04.980 --> 00:13:07.420
I'll just draw it and
then I will write it down.

00:13:07.420 --> 00:13:10.290
I can write it down in all
sorts of very cool ways.

00:13:10.290 --> 00:13:10.930
There we go.

00:13:10.930 --> 00:13:13.140
And then I just do this.

00:13:13.140 --> 00:13:17.040
So this thing, that's the
so-called convex envelope

00:13:17.040 --> 00:13:18.960
of the original function.

00:13:18.960 --> 00:13:20.380
And it makes sense.

00:13:20.380 --> 00:13:22.200
So lots of ways to say it.

00:13:22.200 --> 00:13:26.340
It's the largest convex
function that is everywhere

00:13:26.340 --> 00:13:28.840
below your given function.

00:13:28.840 --> 00:13:30.480
So that's another way to say it.

00:13:30.480 --> 00:13:32.830
Here's a super compact
description of it,

00:13:32.830 --> 00:13:34.420
which is very cool.

00:13:34.420 --> 00:13:36.690
It's f star star.

00:13:36.690 --> 00:13:40.320
So it's the conjugate
of the conjugate.

00:13:40.320 --> 00:13:44.340
Remember that the conjugate
of any function convex or not

00:13:44.340 --> 00:13:45.180
is convex.

00:13:48.010 --> 00:13:50.110
Then when I take the
conjugate of that,

00:13:50.110 --> 00:13:52.120
if the original
function had been convex

00:13:52.120 --> 00:13:54.070
I would have recovered
the initial function.

00:13:54.070 --> 00:13:56.612
But I'm definitely going to get
something that is-- actually,

00:13:56.612 --> 00:13:58.653
you can easily show you're
going to get something

00:13:58.653 --> 00:14:01.420
which is a lower bound on the
original function, number one.

00:14:01.420 --> 00:14:04.520
So f star star is less
than f is always true.

00:14:04.520 --> 00:14:07.810
But the other cool part
is had it been convex,

00:14:07.810 --> 00:14:09.768
you would have gotten f back.

00:14:09.768 --> 00:14:12.310
So that's actually another way
to express the envelope, which

00:14:12.310 --> 00:14:13.285
I think is cool.

00:14:16.290 --> 00:14:18.400
And in fact, you'll
see a lot of people

00:14:18.400 --> 00:14:21.430
not even use fn but
just literally write

00:14:21.430 --> 00:14:24.280
f star star in papers
as the convex envelope.

00:14:24.280 --> 00:14:25.660
So it makes sense.

00:14:25.660 --> 00:14:29.530
And you can work it out
for at least some things.

00:14:29.530 --> 00:14:33.250
And we can actually
work it out for the--

00:14:33.250 --> 00:14:35.890
we can work it out
for the card function

00:14:35.890 --> 00:14:38.750
but restricted to an interval.

00:14:38.750 --> 00:14:41.410
So I'm going to write
down the card function.

00:14:41.410 --> 00:14:42.790
This is 0.

00:14:42.790 --> 00:14:43.990
So, let's see.

00:14:43.990 --> 00:14:45.220
It looks like this--

00:14:45.220 --> 00:14:46.613
oh, no, so that's filled.

00:14:46.613 --> 00:14:47.780
And then it looks like that.

00:14:47.780 --> 00:14:50.030
That's the card function.

00:14:50.030 --> 00:14:52.220
By the way, what is
the convex envelope

00:14:52.220 --> 00:14:54.020
of just card by itself?

00:14:54.020 --> 00:14:56.135
It has one and I'd like
to know what it is.

00:14:59.080 --> 00:14:59.800
It is 0.

00:14:59.800 --> 00:15:00.370
Yeah.

00:15:00.370 --> 00:15:01.350
This is 0.

00:15:01.350 --> 00:15:02.800
It's the constant 0.

00:15:02.800 --> 00:15:07.175
And you go-- if you think about
it, at A, it's convex, and B,

00:15:07.175 --> 00:15:08.800
it's a lower bound
on the card function

00:15:08.800 --> 00:15:11.090
because a card function
only has the value 0 or 1.

00:15:11.090 --> 00:15:12.280
So congratulations.

00:15:12.280 --> 00:15:13.405
It's completely useless.

00:15:16.360 --> 00:15:18.610
But if we say,
no, you know what?

00:15:18.610 --> 00:15:23.510
But I happen to also know
that the variable is between,

00:15:23.510 --> 00:15:28.340
let's say, minus R and
plus R, so the actual

00:15:28.340 --> 00:15:30.340
function I'm taking the
card of looks like this.

00:15:30.340 --> 00:15:33.430
And it goes up to plus
infinity out there.

00:15:33.430 --> 00:15:36.400
What is the convex
envelope of that function?

00:15:36.400 --> 00:15:39.140
This is actually very cool.

00:15:39.140 --> 00:15:41.140
You can get it.

00:15:41.140 --> 00:15:41.770
What is it?

00:15:41.770 --> 00:15:42.730
[INAUDIBLE]

00:15:42.730 --> 00:15:43.300
Yeah.

00:15:43.300 --> 00:15:47.110
So you just draw and
you go like this.

00:15:47.110 --> 00:15:49.330
Literally, you
take the epigraph--

00:15:49.330 --> 00:15:52.540
the epigraph of the whole thing
is this thing up here going up

00:15:52.540 --> 00:15:55.250
to infinity, plus there's a
little needle sticking down

00:15:55.250 --> 00:15:55.750
here.

00:15:55.750 --> 00:15:56.920
That's the epigraph.

00:15:56.920 --> 00:16:00.350
I take the convex hull of the
epigraph that fills in this.

00:16:00.350 --> 00:16:04.700
And then this is f star star.

00:16:04.700 --> 00:16:08.980
So, in this case,
where f is card

00:16:08.980 --> 00:16:11.650
restricted to
absolute value less

00:16:11.650 --> 00:16:14.840
than R. Everybody got this?

00:16:14.840 --> 00:16:17.870
Actually, it's cool because it
suggests all sorts of things.

00:16:17.870 --> 00:16:21.230
If you had other bounds
on x that were asymmetric,

00:16:21.230 --> 00:16:23.990
you would use an
asymmetric thing here.

00:16:23.990 --> 00:16:27.200
And by the way, it would
be a better sparsifier.

00:16:27.200 --> 00:16:31.310
So this is the idea.

00:16:31.310 --> 00:16:33.660
So, actually, you don't
have to worry about that.

00:16:33.660 --> 00:16:35.930
So, for example,
in the application

00:16:35.930 --> 00:16:39.110
of regressor selection
in machine learning

00:16:39.110 --> 00:16:41.810
and statistics, this is
generally not a problem

00:16:41.810 --> 00:16:44.570
because the data have--

00:16:44.570 --> 00:16:48.650
I assume standard practice
is to actually standardize

00:16:48.650 --> 00:16:49.440
the features.

00:16:49.440 --> 00:16:53.190
So they're all now
between reasonable ranges.

00:16:53.190 --> 00:16:55.610
In fact, for that
matter roughly speaking

00:16:55.610 --> 00:16:58.370
between plus and minus
1, and so that justifies

00:16:58.370 --> 00:17:00.320
the use of an l1 norm.

00:17:00.320 --> 00:17:05.119
If for whatever reason you did
not standardize your features

00:17:05.119 --> 00:17:07.190
and you want to do
something like Lasso,

00:17:07.190 --> 00:17:09.170
then you should--
well, number one,

00:17:09.170 --> 00:17:11.000
you should not do
that, number one.

00:17:11.000 --> 00:17:14.030
But if you did do
that, you should

00:17:14.030 --> 00:17:18.109
be using a weird weighted l1
norm or an asymmetric thing

00:17:18.109 --> 00:17:19.700
or something like that.

00:17:19.700 --> 00:17:22.250
Everybody following this?

00:17:22.250 --> 00:17:23.150
Good.

00:17:23.150 --> 00:17:24.470
It's interesting.

00:17:24.470 --> 00:17:26.990
OK.

00:17:26.990 --> 00:17:28.145
So that's just this.

00:17:28.145 --> 00:17:32.150
If you knew, for example, that--

00:17:32.150 --> 00:17:38.120
if you knew that over this
convex feasible set x--

00:17:38.120 --> 00:17:41.090
if you calculated what's
called the bounding box,

00:17:41.090 --> 00:17:43.310
which by the way I could
solve for any problem

00:17:43.310 --> 00:17:46.040
if I don't mind solving
2n convex problems.

00:17:46.040 --> 00:17:49.970
I just maximize xi subject to x
and C, and then I minimize xi.

00:17:49.970 --> 00:17:51.470
Those are both convex problems.

00:17:51.470 --> 00:17:55.680
And I will get the best
possible values of l and u.

00:17:55.680 --> 00:17:57.870
If x were-- let's see.

00:17:57.870 --> 00:18:01.950
If x has a dimension
really small like 10,

00:18:01.950 --> 00:18:04.080
you should just consider
solving it brute force

00:18:04.080 --> 00:18:10.980
and just solve all 1,024
convex problems, then is 20.

00:18:10.980 --> 00:18:13.410
This is actually
interesting right here.

00:18:13.410 --> 00:18:16.350
Because it's not going to kill
you to solve 40 problems to get

00:18:16.350 --> 00:18:19.860
the bounds, which is going to
give you a better sparsifier

00:18:19.860 --> 00:18:21.900
than just an l1 norm.

00:18:21.900 --> 00:18:23.940
So that would be one.

00:18:23.940 --> 00:18:27.480
What you get though is
something really cool.

00:18:27.480 --> 00:18:29.968
You get a function
that matches--

00:18:29.968 --> 00:18:31.260
it's just what you think it is.

00:18:31.260 --> 00:18:32.970
I'll just draw the
picture over here.

00:18:32.970 --> 00:18:36.540
It just says, suppose
this is between--

00:18:36.540 --> 00:18:40.860
what I call this? li or
something and then this is ui.

00:18:40.860 --> 00:18:42.030
I'll make it asymmetric.

00:18:42.030 --> 00:18:45.000
There's ui.

00:18:45.000 --> 00:18:47.580
I'll just call it
u. i doesn't matter.

00:18:47.580 --> 00:18:51.930
Then your-- I mean,
this is clear.

00:18:51.930 --> 00:18:56.560
But your convex envelope
looks like this.

00:18:56.560 --> 00:19:01.330
And it's this tilted function.

00:19:01.330 --> 00:19:04.570
Actually, which I
think people now

00:19:04.570 --> 00:19:08.602
call the pinball
loss because I think

00:19:08.602 --> 00:19:10.060
it has to do with
pinball machines.

00:19:10.060 --> 00:19:10.570
Don't ask.

00:19:10.570 --> 00:19:11.893
Anyway, fine.

00:19:11.893 --> 00:19:12.685
I think it's stuck.

00:19:15.730 --> 00:19:18.400
That's what this is called.

00:19:18.400 --> 00:19:19.900
So this is the idea.

00:19:19.900 --> 00:19:25.765
And if you do this, you'll
get a better sparsifier,

00:19:25.765 --> 00:19:28.600
its principle.

00:19:28.600 --> 00:19:31.390
Now we'll do some examples.

00:19:31.390 --> 00:19:33.500
The first one is
regressor selection.

00:19:33.500 --> 00:19:35.770
So here we're doing
least squares,

00:19:35.770 --> 00:19:42.200
but we want to choose here
which of the entries--

00:19:42.200 --> 00:19:44.230
so x represents our
parameter in our model

00:19:44.230 --> 00:19:48.940
and we want it to
have just k nonzeros.

00:19:48.940 --> 00:19:51.040
So it basically says,
here's an example.

00:19:51.040 --> 00:19:55.270
I'd say, here's 25,000
gene expressions.

00:19:55.270 --> 00:19:58.750
I'd like you to fit a model
that predicts some outcome

00:19:58.750 --> 00:20:00.460
for my 600 patients.

00:20:00.460 --> 00:20:03.310
You'd say, please--
or my 600 experiments

00:20:03.310 --> 00:20:05.170
with mice or something.

00:20:05.170 --> 00:20:07.660
And I'd say, you know what?

00:20:07.660 --> 00:20:09.550
You can use 30 of them.

00:20:09.550 --> 00:20:10.852
Go ahead.

00:20:10.852 --> 00:20:12.310
And the whole point
there is 25,000

00:20:12.310 --> 00:20:15.010
choose 30 is a
pretty big number.

00:20:15.010 --> 00:20:19.760
So this would be an example
of a regressor selection.

00:20:19.760 --> 00:20:20.260
OK.

00:20:20.260 --> 00:20:21.410
There are lots of
ways to do this.

00:20:21.410 --> 00:20:23.123
And they're all related
to Lasso and they

00:20:23.123 --> 00:20:24.790
have all sorts of
other names and things

00:20:24.790 --> 00:20:26.590
like that like basis pursuit.

00:20:26.590 --> 00:20:29.320
It depends on whether it's a
constraint or a regularizer

00:20:29.320 --> 00:20:31.120
and all that kind of stuff.

00:20:31.120 --> 00:20:33.190
But this is cool.

00:20:33.190 --> 00:20:35.080
And here's just a
quick baby example.

00:20:39.010 --> 00:20:40.910
Here's x and R20.

00:20:40.910 --> 00:20:42.910
So that means, of course,
that there's a million

00:20:42.910 --> 00:20:45.620
different possible values of x.

00:20:45.620 --> 00:20:49.100
Sorry, sparsity patterns
for x is a million.

00:20:49.100 --> 00:20:52.550
So we just solved it globally
because it's not a big deal.

00:20:52.550 --> 00:20:56.870
And then you get a
trade-off curve of--

00:20:56.870 --> 00:20:58.670
this is how many
features you use.

00:20:58.670 --> 00:21:02.300
And then this is how small
you can make norm x minus b.

00:21:02.300 --> 00:21:03.950
This might be some kind of fit.

00:21:03.950 --> 00:21:05.360
And there's a trade-off.

00:21:05.360 --> 00:21:09.380
This one shows you the--
this is the global optimal.

00:21:09.380 --> 00:21:12.590
This would have been impossible
had this been our R50.

00:21:12.590 --> 00:21:15.440
I mean, not
impossible because it

00:21:15.440 --> 00:21:19.430
would be outside the
stuff we do in this class.

00:21:19.430 --> 00:21:22.340
So if you do the l1--

00:21:22.340 --> 00:21:25.640
if you do the l1
heuristic with polishing,

00:21:25.640 --> 00:21:27.080
you actually get this--

00:21:27.080 --> 00:21:27.710
this thing.

00:21:27.710 --> 00:21:29.180
And it's actually really cool.

00:21:29.180 --> 00:21:30.440
Often, you're just dead on.

00:21:30.440 --> 00:21:32.000
It's like here it's dead on.

00:21:32.000 --> 00:21:34.610
Well, that's silly, but
here you're dead on.

00:21:34.610 --> 00:21:40.460
Here, now you're off by
1, here you're off by 1.

00:21:40.460 --> 00:21:45.500
It's actually interesting
how this works.

00:21:45.500 --> 00:21:46.000
All right.

00:21:46.000 --> 00:21:49.960
So this is a baby example but
it's just to illustrate this.

00:21:49.960 --> 00:21:54.130
A huge, big difference that
the approximate difference

00:21:54.130 --> 00:21:57.135
in computation
effort, in this case,

00:21:57.135 --> 00:21:58.760
we could have done
it more efficiently.

00:21:58.760 --> 00:22:03.130
But, in this case,
it's 1 million to 1.

00:22:03.130 --> 00:22:08.265
Because each of this point,
this point was found--

00:22:08.265 --> 00:22:09.640
maybe not a million
but whatever,

00:22:09.640 --> 00:22:11.740
this point was found--

00:22:11.740 --> 00:22:13.720
actually, it is 1
million to 1 roughly,

00:22:13.720 --> 00:22:17.890
this point was found by
actually checking all 20 choose

00:22:17.890 --> 00:22:20.950
for sparsity
patterns and finding

00:22:20.950 --> 00:22:23.650
the one that had the best fit.

00:22:23.650 --> 00:22:26.290
So it's not a small
number, whatever it is.

00:22:26.290 --> 00:22:26.960
So that's it.

00:22:26.960 --> 00:22:28.570
So the idea that
you get this close

00:22:28.570 --> 00:22:32.690
with such a stunning
reduction in computation

00:22:32.690 --> 00:22:34.880
that you can't claim
that you've actually

00:22:34.880 --> 00:22:39.160
solved the original cardinality
problem, but that's OK.

00:22:39.160 --> 00:22:40.315
Yes.

00:22:40.315 --> 00:22:44.290
How do you know for sure that
the components that are not 0

00:22:44.290 --> 00:22:47.860
are the same in both cases?

00:22:47.860 --> 00:22:49.000
Oh, we don't.

00:22:49.000 --> 00:22:49.990
In fact, they're not.

00:22:49.990 --> 00:22:54.430
Because, let's see, which
one are you looking at?

00:22:54.430 --> 00:22:57.430
The last one when
they maps [INAUDIBLE]..

00:22:57.430 --> 00:22:58.105
You mean here?

00:22:58.105 --> 00:22:58.605
Yeah.

00:22:58.605 --> 00:22:59.380
Yeah.

00:22:59.380 --> 00:23:02.500
Well, a was probably
just chosen randomly.

00:23:02.500 --> 00:23:05.572
So that has probability
0 of happening.

00:23:05.572 --> 00:23:06.280
Of course, it is.

00:23:06.280 --> 00:23:08.440
I mean, it's not
necessarily true.

00:23:08.440 --> 00:23:12.910
If a had two columns
that were identical,

00:23:12.910 --> 00:23:15.130
or if you want to translate
it to machine learning

00:23:15.130 --> 00:23:16.990
and fitting, you'd
say two features which

00:23:16.990 --> 00:23:20.260
are either 100% correlated
or exactly the same,

00:23:20.260 --> 00:23:21.760
then in fact, you
get your choice.

00:23:21.760 --> 00:23:22.885
They might not be the same.

00:23:25.380 --> 00:23:26.850
And I don't think that matters.

00:23:26.850 --> 00:23:29.580
We're just saying
what is the best--

00:23:29.580 --> 00:23:33.130
this says you may pick
four features and it says,

00:23:33.130 --> 00:23:35.670
what's the best fit you can get?

00:23:35.670 --> 00:23:38.697
Usually, there's a unique set
of four features that does that.

00:23:38.697 --> 00:23:40.780
But if there's not, then
I don't think it matters.

00:23:40.780 --> 00:23:41.715
It's the same thing.

00:23:44.370 --> 00:23:46.480
I don't know if that made sense.

00:23:46.480 --> 00:23:50.140
Didn't totally make sense to
me, but anyway, that's fine.

00:23:53.810 --> 00:23:56.210
Here's sparse signal
reconstruction.

00:23:58.990 --> 00:24:02.380
So these are just
the standard things.

00:24:02.380 --> 00:24:05.530
Actually, a lot of
people would square this.

00:24:05.530 --> 00:24:08.260
It has no effect it changes
of course the parameterization

00:24:08.260 --> 00:24:11.860
with the hyperparameter gamma,
but otherwise, it's the same.

00:24:11.860 --> 00:24:14.470
In fact, those are
two scalarizations

00:24:14.470 --> 00:24:17.320
of what's really a
bicriterion problem.

00:24:17.320 --> 00:24:19.297
So these are one.

00:24:19.297 --> 00:24:20.380
We got all sorts of names.

00:24:20.380 --> 00:24:21.880
Here's just a quick example.

00:24:21.880 --> 00:24:24.796
So there's a signal with a--

00:24:24.796 --> 00:24:28.420
it's got a dimension of 1,000,
but there's only 30 nonzeros.

00:24:28.420 --> 00:24:29.770
And here's the exact signal.

00:24:29.770 --> 00:24:33.700
This is just a
simulated example.

00:24:33.700 --> 00:24:38.050
And then I give you 200
random noisy measurements

00:24:38.050 --> 00:24:41.950
of these 1,000 numbers.

00:24:41.950 --> 00:24:46.120
And so if you do an
l1 reconstruction

00:24:46.120 --> 00:24:49.900
with an appropriately
chosen gamma,

00:24:49.900 --> 00:24:52.780
you basically get it exactly.

00:24:52.780 --> 00:24:59.020
So I think 20 years ago when
people were first seeing this,

00:24:59.020 --> 00:25:01.870
this was pretty stunning.

00:25:01.870 --> 00:25:03.430
Nowadays, I think
a lot of people

00:25:03.430 --> 00:25:05.320
have already seen this in
undergraduate machine learning

00:25:05.320 --> 00:25:07.090
classes and they're
like yeah, so what?

00:25:07.090 --> 00:25:07.850
Who cares?

00:25:07.850 --> 00:25:12.100
But you really should stop
and actually reflect on this

00:25:12.100 --> 00:25:13.840
and think how cool--
this is basically

00:25:13.840 --> 00:25:16.820
somebody walks up to you
on the street and says,

00:25:16.820 --> 00:25:20.580
I have 1,000 numbers I'd
like to help you figure out.

00:25:20.580 --> 00:25:21.430
You go, cool.

00:25:21.430 --> 00:25:22.870
I'll be happy to help.

00:25:22.870 --> 00:25:25.520
You tell me what
measurements you have.

00:25:25.520 --> 00:25:27.790
You go, I have 200 measurements.

00:25:27.790 --> 00:25:31.600
You're like, oh, I'm
sorry, did you say 2,000?

00:25:31.600 --> 00:25:34.090
Or did you get the
two numbers different?

00:25:34.090 --> 00:25:36.280
Do you have 1,000
measurements and something

00:25:36.280 --> 00:25:38.870
like that and 300 parameters
you want to estimate?

00:25:38.870 --> 00:25:42.430
You go, no, I would like to
estimate 1,000 parameters based

00:25:42.430 --> 00:25:44.260
on 300 noisy measurements.

00:25:44.260 --> 00:25:47.020
So the correct thing to do
in a situation like that

00:25:47.020 --> 00:25:49.810
is to walk away
from that person.

00:25:49.810 --> 00:25:53.470
Maybe not turn your back on
them, just walk away carefully

00:25:53.470 --> 00:25:54.907
and avoid them from then on.

00:25:54.907 --> 00:25:56.990
It's like someone walks
up to the street and says,

00:25:56.990 --> 00:25:59.780
I'm thinking of four numbers
they add to 12, what are they?

00:25:59.780 --> 00:26:01.610
You should avoid
people like this

00:26:01.610 --> 00:26:03.830
because it's a
nonsensical problem.

00:26:03.830 --> 00:26:04.490
OK.

00:26:04.490 --> 00:26:05.150
Fine.

00:26:05.150 --> 00:26:08.960
But then they say, ah,
but only 30 are nonzero.

00:26:08.960 --> 00:26:13.400
Then you'd say, that sounds
like a normal thing to me.

00:26:13.400 --> 00:26:15.770
Because you think
of fitting and stuff

00:26:15.770 --> 00:26:19.400
like that like data
compression and you go, cool.

00:26:19.400 --> 00:26:22.340
I have 300 noisy measurements
to estimate, is that right?

00:26:22.340 --> 00:26:24.050
Yeah, 200 noisy
measurements estimate

00:26:24.050 --> 00:26:26.090
30 things, that feels right.

00:26:26.090 --> 00:26:27.500
That's a sensible thing.

00:26:27.500 --> 00:26:28.790
You'd say, what are you doing?

00:26:28.790 --> 00:26:29.623
I'm fitting a model.

00:26:29.623 --> 00:26:31.207
How many parameters
are in your model?

00:26:31.207 --> 00:26:31.780
30.

00:26:31.780 --> 00:26:32.630
How many measurements you got?

00:26:32.630 --> 00:26:33.320
200.

00:26:33.320 --> 00:26:34.400
Sounds totally cool.

00:26:34.400 --> 00:26:35.610
OK.

00:26:35.610 --> 00:26:37.350
But there's a catch.

00:26:37.350 --> 00:26:43.290
No one tells you which
30 of the 1,000 they are.

00:26:43.290 --> 00:26:46.210
And 1,000 choose
30 is a big number.

00:26:46.210 --> 00:26:47.860
Everybody following this?

00:26:47.860 --> 00:26:48.360
OK.

00:26:48.360 --> 00:26:50.790
So what's amazing
is it just works.

00:26:50.790 --> 00:26:53.700
And I think one of the first
packages for this maybe 20

00:26:53.700 --> 00:26:57.540
years ago, 25 years ago
was called L1-MAGIC,

00:26:57.540 --> 00:27:00.570
was the name for it, which
was now people don't care.

00:27:00.570 --> 00:27:03.300
But it is good every now and
then think how crazy this

00:27:03.300 --> 00:27:05.850
is that this actually works.

00:27:05.850 --> 00:27:07.140
OK.

00:27:07.140 --> 00:27:11.220
Just for comparison, that
would be the l2 reconstruction.

00:27:11.220 --> 00:27:12.240
Well, duh.

00:27:12.240 --> 00:27:13.710
If I walk up to
you and say please

00:27:13.710 --> 00:27:15.330
help me guess
1,000 numbers, I'll

00:27:15.330 --> 00:27:17.013
give you 300 noisy
measurements, what

00:27:17.013 --> 00:27:18.930
did you think you were
going to get-- what did

00:27:18.930 --> 00:27:20.790
you think was going to happen?

00:27:20.790 --> 00:27:22.860
So this fits with that.

00:27:22.860 --> 00:27:24.530
OK.

00:27:24.530 --> 00:27:27.500
I'm actually going to skip
this because, I don't know,

00:27:27.500 --> 00:27:30.050
these fussy things
that actually tell you

00:27:30.050 --> 00:27:31.490
under some circumstances.

00:27:31.490 --> 00:27:35.850
This actually does with high
probability actually work.

00:27:35.850 --> 00:27:38.630
I'm going to go on from that.

00:27:38.630 --> 00:27:40.640
We'll just go to the next part.

00:27:48.900 --> 00:27:49.400
OK.

00:27:49.400 --> 00:27:53.820
So we'll look at a couple more
examples that are pretty cool.

00:27:53.820 --> 00:27:57.140
So you could do the same thing.

00:27:57.140 --> 00:28:00.830
We did total variation
for a scalar signal.

00:28:00.830 --> 00:28:02.660
So the total variation
is essentially

00:28:02.660 --> 00:28:04.070
the l1 norm of the derivative.

00:28:04.070 --> 00:28:06.650
That's rough, but
that's what it is.

00:28:06.650 --> 00:28:09.260
It tells you how much--

00:28:09.260 --> 00:28:10.400
well, that's what it is.

00:28:10.400 --> 00:28:12.500
Let's just leave it that way.

00:28:12.500 --> 00:28:16.280
And the convex
cardinality thing,

00:28:16.280 --> 00:28:19.700
that's called a total variation,
you do the same thing.

00:28:19.700 --> 00:28:22.220
And we actually saw an example.

00:28:22.220 --> 00:28:24.830
I think here's an
example where the top is

00:28:24.830 --> 00:28:27.800
some original signal which
has got smooth parts but also

00:28:27.800 --> 00:28:30.700
these big jumps.

00:28:30.700 --> 00:28:32.985
The corrupted version is below.

00:28:32.985 --> 00:28:34.360
And then when you
denoise it, you

00:28:34.360 --> 00:28:38.050
get things like this
using l1 reconstruction.

00:28:38.050 --> 00:28:40.150
And you could, I
don't know, you could

00:28:40.150 --> 00:28:43.210
decide which value
is right, which

00:28:43.210 --> 00:28:46.030
you could do by some
cross-validation or something.

00:28:46.030 --> 00:28:49.750
But roughly speaking, this
looks like it's overregularized.

00:28:49.750 --> 00:28:52.930
That looks like maybe
it's underregularized.

00:28:52.930 --> 00:28:55.780
And you might say that that's
about right or maybe somewhere

00:28:55.780 --> 00:28:58.105
even in between these
two might be about right.

00:29:00.970 --> 00:29:03.580
And so this is
used in-- actually,

00:29:03.580 --> 00:29:05.810
there was a famous
incident in the '90s.

00:29:05.810 --> 00:29:09.880
I think this was used
to reconstruct actually

00:29:09.880 --> 00:29:15.430
a lot of old wax recordings
of Caruso, the opera singer.

00:29:15.430 --> 00:29:19.810
They would have these wax
drums all scratched up

00:29:19.810 --> 00:29:21.460
and things like that.

00:29:21.460 --> 00:29:25.270
And to extract-- what something
based on the total variation

00:29:25.270 --> 00:29:27.430
denoising would
do would actually

00:29:27.430 --> 00:29:30.380
remove a lot of the
noise but actually

00:29:30.380 --> 00:29:32.570
preserve the sharp attacks.

00:29:32.570 --> 00:29:35.240
Because, otherwise, if you did
some of the l2 version of it,

00:29:35.240 --> 00:29:36.920
it's just a low-pass filter.

00:29:36.920 --> 00:29:38.760
I'm now speaking
dialect, but sorry.

00:29:38.760 --> 00:29:41.390
And so basically, it would
just come out muffled.

00:29:41.390 --> 00:29:44.930
Good news, noise is
substantially reduced,

00:29:44.930 --> 00:29:47.720
bad news, so is
the actual content.

00:29:47.720 --> 00:29:49.550
But when you do a
total variation,

00:29:49.550 --> 00:29:51.880
you get something
that's actually cool.

00:29:51.880 --> 00:29:55.370
The attack of a drum
beat when somebody

00:29:55.370 --> 00:29:58.160
leans into a snare
drum, it's right there.

00:29:58.160 --> 00:30:02.360
So, anyway, that was in the
'90s, I think, which was cool.

00:30:02.360 --> 00:30:03.440
OK.

00:30:03.440 --> 00:30:06.550
I will skip this because
I think we saw that.

00:30:06.550 --> 00:30:10.240
One more example,
but this is in 2D.

00:30:10.240 --> 00:30:12.710
And even this is
a simplification.

00:30:12.710 --> 00:30:16.165
And I will make a comment
about this a bit later.

00:30:20.420 --> 00:30:23.750
Let's say that I have
an image or let's

00:30:23.750 --> 00:30:26.300
say I'm doing MRI
reconstruction.

00:30:26.300 --> 00:30:27.965
And so I have all
these values here.

00:30:31.340 --> 00:30:34.190
So here the total variation
is actually given by--

00:30:34.190 --> 00:30:38.780
I mean, in the continuous
version, it's actually this.

00:30:38.780 --> 00:30:43.370
It's f of-- here,
I can call this--

00:30:43.370 --> 00:30:44.750
yeah, I'll do this.

00:30:44.750 --> 00:30:45.485
It's this.

00:30:48.020 --> 00:30:49.961
There you go.

00:30:49.961 --> 00:30:54.530
That is what the
total variation is

00:30:54.530 --> 00:30:58.100
when you do in the
infinite-dimensional case.

00:30:58.100 --> 00:31:01.785
Very important here,
this is not squared.

00:31:01.785 --> 00:31:03.660
So as a matter of fact,
there's a weird thing

00:31:03.660 --> 00:31:05.220
when you show this to someone.

00:31:05.220 --> 00:31:07.800
It's like if you
show someone d, d,

00:31:07.800 --> 00:31:10.050
they just don't
even see it, often.

00:31:10.050 --> 00:31:14.090
So many people when shown
this, their retina--

00:31:14.090 --> 00:31:17.520
I didn't even know where it
happens in the optical cortex,

00:31:17.520 --> 00:31:19.663
but a 2 appears here.

00:31:19.663 --> 00:31:22.330
And then you have to go-- so you
have to actually emphasize this

00:31:22.330 --> 00:31:24.163
when you show this to
people because they're

00:31:24.163 --> 00:31:25.162
like, yeah, sure, sure.

00:31:25.162 --> 00:31:26.370
Then they go to the next one.

00:31:26.370 --> 00:31:30.343
And something paints in a
2 here just because that's

00:31:30.343 --> 00:31:31.260
the traditional thing.

00:31:31.260 --> 00:31:32.460
You don't paint it in here.

00:31:32.460 --> 00:31:34.960
So that's total variation.

00:31:34.960 --> 00:31:35.460
OK.

00:31:35.460 --> 00:31:41.460
So simple version of that
here is to estimate this,

00:31:41.460 --> 00:31:46.440
we make a two vector,
which is the x gradient

00:31:46.440 --> 00:31:50.417
is just the difference here
and the y gradient is this.

00:31:50.417 --> 00:31:52.500
It turns out that's actually
not the way to do it.

00:31:52.500 --> 00:31:54.720
You get artifacts that
align with the axes.

00:31:54.720 --> 00:31:56.250
And there's actually
way cooler ways

00:31:56.250 --> 00:31:59.460
to estimate the gradient
based on, well, I don't know,

00:31:59.460 --> 00:32:02.380
if you study PDEs or something,
you get a little template.

00:32:02.380 --> 00:32:05.850
And you make a gradient here
that depends on these things.

00:32:05.850 --> 00:32:07.890
I think they call that
a stencil or something.

00:32:07.890 --> 00:32:09.480
And there's some
coefficients in there

00:32:09.480 --> 00:32:12.900
to map those five values
to two gradient numbers.

00:32:12.900 --> 00:32:15.960
This is the space x gradient
and the space y gradient.

00:32:15.960 --> 00:32:17.190
Everybody got this?

00:32:17.190 --> 00:32:21.360
And you can make those so that
they are actually approximately

00:32:21.360 --> 00:32:22.888
rotation invariant.

00:32:22.888 --> 00:32:24.180
So you don't get the artifacts.

00:32:24.180 --> 00:32:25.770
I only mention this.

00:32:25.770 --> 00:32:28.420
This is the main idea here.

00:32:28.420 --> 00:32:33.090
This is the crudest possible
stencil or approximation

00:32:33.090 --> 00:32:36.400
of the space
gradient of an image

00:32:36.400 --> 00:32:38.830
would be its x differences
and its y differences.

00:32:38.830 --> 00:32:40.300
But you could do
actually better.

00:32:40.300 --> 00:32:40.660
OK.

00:32:40.660 --> 00:32:43.000
So you get something that
looks like that with that very

00:32:43.000 --> 00:32:43.900
crude approximation.

00:32:43.900 --> 00:32:45.483
You get something
that looks like this

00:32:45.483 --> 00:32:48.940
and then you end up with
something like that.

00:32:48.940 --> 00:32:50.290
OK.

00:32:50.290 --> 00:32:53.650
So we'll look at
an example of this.

00:32:53.650 --> 00:32:56.950
So here's what
happens is I have--

00:32:56.950 --> 00:32:59.200
this is probably
tomography or something.

00:32:59.200 --> 00:33:03.100
So this is my
original thing in 2D.

00:33:03.100 --> 00:33:05.120
I think it's a
tomography problem.

00:33:05.120 --> 00:33:09.340
So I calculate line
integrals through it

00:33:09.340 --> 00:33:11.360
and then I reconstruct it.

00:33:11.360 --> 00:33:15.415
And if you use total
variation to do this--

00:33:18.213 --> 00:33:19.630
and this is again
a case where you

00:33:19.630 --> 00:33:21.460
have 8 eight times
more variables

00:33:21.460 --> 00:33:22.970
to predict than measurements.

00:33:22.970 --> 00:33:24.390
So it's the same
as the last one.

00:33:24.390 --> 00:33:26.432
You just reproduce it like
pretty much perfectly.

00:33:26.432 --> 00:33:27.973
Actually, if you
look closely, you'll

00:33:27.973 --> 00:33:29.500
see that there are
weird artifacts,

00:33:29.500 --> 00:33:31.420
but that would be fixed
if you did a fancier

00:33:31.420 --> 00:33:33.730
version of this approximation.

00:33:33.730 --> 00:33:36.820
And again, if you just
wanted to compare that,

00:33:36.820 --> 00:33:40.420
that's what the l2
reconstruction would look like.

00:33:40.420 --> 00:33:42.505
So it's not bad.

00:33:42.505 --> 00:33:45.010
It knows that there were
some line integrals that

00:33:45.010 --> 00:33:47.740
went through the main part of
it that came out pretty big.

00:33:47.740 --> 00:33:49.690
And from that,
you would conclude

00:33:49.690 --> 00:33:51.340
there's something
there in the middle

00:33:51.340 --> 00:33:54.010
and it's basically a blob.

00:33:54.010 --> 00:33:56.590
So this is standard.

00:33:56.590 --> 00:33:58.060
A lot of people do
other stuff now,

00:33:58.060 --> 00:34:04.120
but these were regularizers
that were a lot better than what

00:34:04.120 --> 00:34:04.885
people had before.

00:34:10.219 --> 00:34:12.230
We're now going to mention
a little bit about--

00:34:12.230 --> 00:34:16.429
just for fun, the idea of an
iterated weighted l1 heuristic.

00:34:16.429 --> 00:34:19.110
And the way that
works is this is

00:34:19.110 --> 00:34:23.510
I start with all the weights
one here and I do l1.

00:34:23.510 --> 00:34:25.715
Then what I do is
actually really cool.

00:34:29.270 --> 00:34:32.719
If xi is 0 here, I set the
weight to 1 over epsilon.

00:34:32.719 --> 00:34:35.750
Epsilon is a small number and
that means it's really high.

00:34:35.750 --> 00:34:41.420
If x is big, I actually
reduce the weight below 1.

00:34:41.420 --> 00:34:45.110
So basically, what this
is doing this update

00:34:45.110 --> 00:34:47.322
here is actually doing triage.

00:34:47.322 --> 00:34:48.739
It's basically
who's going to live

00:34:48.739 --> 00:34:50.510
and who's not going to live.

00:34:50.510 --> 00:34:55.790
So if you're small or
0, your weight goes up

00:34:55.790 --> 00:34:58.580
and that means you're a prime
target for being mapped to 0

00:34:58.580 --> 00:35:00.560
in the next l1 minimization.

00:35:00.560 --> 00:35:03.480
If you're big, your
weight is reduced,

00:35:03.480 --> 00:35:07.010
which means that basically
if I anthropomorphize

00:35:07.010 --> 00:35:10.500
that means you've
concluded that whatever,

00:35:10.500 --> 00:35:13.020
x7 is going to be nonzero.

00:35:13.020 --> 00:35:14.800
And so you reduce the weight.

00:35:14.800 --> 00:35:16.380
This is actually
combines polishing

00:35:16.380 --> 00:35:19.680
there because polishing is
an extreme form of this.

00:35:19.680 --> 00:35:22.320
Polishing goes like this,
solve the l1 problem.

00:35:22.320 --> 00:35:24.580
And you're zero, put an
infinite weight on you.

00:35:24.580 --> 00:35:26.580
And that means you're
never going to be nonzero.

00:35:29.250 --> 00:35:32.310
You're nonzero and you go
put your weight to zero,

00:35:32.310 --> 00:35:34.440
meaning no regularization.

00:35:34.440 --> 00:35:35.320
And then resolve.

00:35:35.320 --> 00:35:38.070
So polishing is a version,
an extreme version of this.

00:35:38.070 --> 00:35:40.740
This is like iterative
soft polishing or something

00:35:40.740 --> 00:35:41.790
weird like that.

00:35:41.790 --> 00:35:42.330
OK.

00:35:42.330 --> 00:35:48.090
So this actually works quite
well for a lot of things.

00:35:48.090 --> 00:35:49.800
Very few people know about this.

00:35:49.800 --> 00:35:50.700
I don't know why.

00:35:50.700 --> 00:35:54.020
But it's true that many
people don't know about it.

00:35:54.020 --> 00:35:56.240
And you get a beautiful
interpretation

00:35:56.240 --> 00:36:01.220
by doing something like
this, which is very cool.

00:36:01.220 --> 00:36:05.090
So let's start with
the actual card

00:36:05.090 --> 00:36:07.350
function which looks like this.

00:36:07.350 --> 00:36:12.180
There's the card function and
someone comes along and says,

00:36:12.180 --> 00:36:15.208
well, I'm going to
use an l1 heuristic.

00:36:15.208 --> 00:36:16.250
And they go, what's that?

00:36:16.250 --> 00:36:18.710
And you go, well,
instead of your function,

00:36:18.710 --> 00:36:23.520
I'm going to use this function.

00:36:23.520 --> 00:36:24.920
How about that?

00:36:24.920 --> 00:36:27.650
And they go, that's not a
very good approximation.

00:36:27.650 --> 00:36:30.320
Yeah, but it seems
to work pretty well.

00:36:30.320 --> 00:36:36.140
So here what you do
instead is you write down.

00:36:36.140 --> 00:36:39.620
You make a non-convex function
that it looks close to this

00:36:39.620 --> 00:36:42.380
and I'm going to write
down what that function is.

00:36:42.380 --> 00:36:43.590
It's going to be this.

00:36:43.590 --> 00:36:45.882
I'm going to make a weird
function that goes like this.

00:36:45.882 --> 00:36:48.020
Actually, over here
it'll be minus--

00:36:48.020 --> 00:36:50.457
I can subtract something
to make it like this.

00:36:50.457 --> 00:36:51.665
It's going to look like that.

00:36:56.880 --> 00:37:01.670
And that you would say
that's a better approximation

00:37:01.670 --> 00:37:02.870
of this cardinality.

00:37:02.870 --> 00:37:04.328
Oh, by the way,
people do this also

00:37:04.328 --> 00:37:05.627
with powers, which is cool.

00:37:05.627 --> 00:37:06.710
So you do the square root.

00:37:06.710 --> 00:37:08.900
That's another one you would--

00:37:08.900 --> 00:37:10.580
here's the real thing.

00:37:10.580 --> 00:37:12.170
Sorry, this is like that.

00:37:12.170 --> 00:37:13.160
That's like that.

00:37:13.160 --> 00:37:16.100
And people would put
a square root here.

00:37:16.100 --> 00:37:18.740
Then they would write extremely
annoying papers about--

00:37:18.740 --> 00:37:23.790
they would say lp approximation
with p equals 0.5,

00:37:23.790 --> 00:37:25.940
which drives me nuts because
there's no such thing

00:37:25.940 --> 00:37:27.410
as an l0.5 norm.

00:37:27.410 --> 00:37:31.940
Oh, even worse, people refer
to cardinality as the l0 norm.

00:37:31.940 --> 00:37:35.450
So has anyone heard that?

00:37:35.450 --> 00:37:36.630
Don't let that stand.

00:37:36.630 --> 00:37:38.660
If you are in a social
situation and someone

00:37:38.660 --> 00:37:42.650
says l1 norm, say
something like, excuse me,

00:37:42.650 --> 00:37:45.400
that's not a norm.

00:37:45.400 --> 00:37:46.780
And if they say,
oh, that's cool.

00:37:46.780 --> 00:37:49.330
Sorry, I thought we were just
friends here or whatever,

00:37:49.330 --> 00:37:52.180
just casual, then you go,
fine, but just to check

00:37:52.180 --> 00:37:53.938
that they know that.

00:37:53.938 --> 00:37:56.230
If they don't, then I don't
leave the room or something

00:37:56.230 --> 00:37:58.750
because you should not hang
out with people like that.

00:37:58.750 --> 00:38:01.530
It's like people who talk
about an overcomplete basis,

00:38:01.530 --> 00:38:03.790
and you're like, what?

00:38:03.790 --> 00:38:07.450
By the way, otherwise
known as a set of vectors.

00:38:07.450 --> 00:38:08.980
So just, anyway, all right.

00:38:08.980 --> 00:38:10.880
Fine.

00:38:10.880 --> 00:38:11.380
All right.

00:38:11.380 --> 00:38:16.420
So it turns out here
you do something--

00:38:16.420 --> 00:38:20.740
you just linearize this
objective at the current point

00:38:20.740 --> 00:38:25.990
and then you get exactly this
iterated reweighted heuristic.

00:38:25.990 --> 00:38:28.300
So you can think
of it as doing a--

00:38:28.300 --> 00:38:31.580
people sometimes call that
sequential convex optimization.

00:38:31.580 --> 00:38:36.430
So that's when you have a
term, a constraint term,

00:38:36.430 --> 00:38:38.600
a function that's not convex.

00:38:38.600 --> 00:38:40.210
And what you do is
you approximate it

00:38:40.210 --> 00:38:44.170
by a convex function at each
step and solve that problem.

00:38:44.170 --> 00:38:46.220
And this is an example of that.

00:38:46.220 --> 00:38:51.010
And this works quite well.

00:38:51.010 --> 00:38:52.255
So that's it.

00:38:52.255 --> 00:38:54.380
And I'll just show you some
quick examples of that.

00:38:54.380 --> 00:38:57.520
So here would be a
baby example, right,

00:38:57.520 --> 00:39:02.320
is we want to find
a sparse solution

00:39:02.320 --> 00:39:05.020
in a polyhedron of a set
of linear inequalities.

00:39:05.020 --> 00:39:08.590
So what we do is we start
with the l1 heuristic

00:39:08.590 --> 00:39:10.330
and you come back
with something that

00:39:10.330 --> 00:39:15.480
has cardinality 44,
whatever, it doesn't matter.

00:39:15.480 --> 00:39:20.685
So if you run this
iterated l1 heuristic,

00:39:20.685 --> 00:39:23.100
these are the iterations,
what you can see is like,

00:39:23.100 --> 00:39:25.050
I don't know, in
three, four steps,

00:39:25.050 --> 00:39:27.480
you've got in four steps,
you've got something

00:39:27.480 --> 00:39:30.543
with a cardinality of 36.

00:39:30.543 --> 00:39:32.460
By the way, for this
problem, for fun, we just

00:39:32.460 --> 00:39:36.063
solved it globally and
the global solution is 32.

00:39:36.063 --> 00:39:37.480
By the way, that
worked perfectly.

00:39:37.480 --> 00:39:38.980
It was a randomly
generated example.

00:39:38.980 --> 00:39:41.620
Because had the global
solution been 36,

00:39:41.620 --> 00:39:43.840
this would be misleading.

00:39:43.840 --> 00:39:45.550
So this tells you everything.

00:39:45.550 --> 00:39:47.080
This example worked
out perfectly

00:39:47.080 --> 00:39:50.620
to say exactly the honest and
correct story, which is this

00:39:50.620 --> 00:39:52.492
is a really good heuristic.

00:39:52.492 --> 00:39:54.950
And if someone says does it
always get the global solution?

00:39:54.950 --> 00:39:55.960
The answer is no.

00:39:55.960 --> 00:39:56.560
It doesn't.

00:39:56.560 --> 00:39:58.060
And here's a perfectly
good example.

00:40:01.090 --> 00:40:04.730
So this is just a
quick example of that.

00:40:04.730 --> 00:40:06.420
So you can use this
in all sorts of--

00:40:06.420 --> 00:40:08.420
I mean, once you understand
about these methods,

00:40:08.420 --> 00:40:10.750
you can use them
in very cool ways.

00:40:10.750 --> 00:40:12.440
So here's a quick example.

00:40:12.440 --> 00:40:13.480
I have an AR model.

00:40:13.480 --> 00:40:15.860
So an autoregressive model.

00:40:15.860 --> 00:40:19.480
And what we're
going to do is we're

00:40:19.480 --> 00:40:22.640
going to assume that the
dynamics of the AR model,

00:40:22.640 --> 00:40:26.050
which is the a and
the b, they changed.

00:40:26.050 --> 00:40:29.260
There's a lot of things for this
where you want to fit a model

00:40:29.260 --> 00:40:32.450
but it changes over time
or something like that.

00:40:32.450 --> 00:40:36.260
And so what you do is actually
something pretty cool.

00:40:36.260 --> 00:40:38.650
It's a weird idea
but here it is.

00:40:38.650 --> 00:40:41.500
What you do is you
start by actually

00:40:41.500 --> 00:40:44.450
assuming that you introduce
a number of variables.

00:40:44.450 --> 00:40:46.240
So if you were just
doing an AR 2 fit,

00:40:46.240 --> 00:40:48.250
a and b would be constant
and they'd be two variables.

00:40:48.250 --> 00:40:50.083
And you'd have a bunch
of data because you'd

00:40:50.083 --> 00:40:52.270
have t equals 100 time
steps or a couple hundred

00:40:52.270 --> 00:40:56.530
and everything would make
sense to fit an AR 2 model.

00:40:56.530 --> 00:40:58.270
But here what you
do if someone says,

00:40:58.270 --> 00:41:00.400
no, no, my AR 2 model
is time-varying.

00:41:00.400 --> 00:41:02.380
And you go, OK, cool.

00:41:02.380 --> 00:41:06.400
So you actually have a different
a and b for each time period.

00:41:06.400 --> 00:41:10.780
Minor problem with that is
you're now hideously overfit,

00:41:10.780 --> 00:41:13.510
at least 2 to 1,
because you've got--

00:41:13.510 --> 00:41:17.950
so it's not just that you
can match something exactly,

00:41:17.950 --> 00:41:22.585
you have to put something,
some prior on a and b.

00:41:22.585 --> 00:41:24.700
You can have lots of priors.

00:41:24.700 --> 00:41:26.710
Here would be one-- a
and b are increasing.

00:41:26.710 --> 00:41:28.390
I just made that up.

00:41:28.390 --> 00:41:29.230
Here's one.

00:41:29.230 --> 00:41:31.990
They vary smoothly, meaning
the sum of the squares

00:41:31.990 --> 00:41:33.700
of the differences is small.

00:41:33.700 --> 00:41:34.750
Everybody got that?

00:41:34.750 --> 00:41:38.830
That would be a smoothly
varying AR model.

00:41:38.830 --> 00:41:40.720
Here's another one.

00:41:40.720 --> 00:41:43.330
You have a piecewise
constant AR model,

00:41:43.330 --> 00:41:46.750
meaning a and b are constants
for various periods,

00:41:46.750 --> 00:41:50.020
then there's some shift and
they assume different values.

00:41:50.020 --> 00:41:51.220
Everybody got this?

00:41:51.220 --> 00:41:53.290
But by now, at this
point in the class,

00:41:53.290 --> 00:41:55.780
you have all the
tools to do like you

00:41:55.780 --> 00:42:00.010
could do all sorts of
crazy stuff with this.

00:42:00.010 --> 00:42:02.150
So you do this.

00:42:02.150 --> 00:42:05.770
So this is your-- this is
basically the regularizer

00:42:05.770 --> 00:42:09.460
and this is the loss.

00:42:09.460 --> 00:42:12.820
So this is just a traditional
square loss or Gaussian,

00:42:12.820 --> 00:42:14.980
whatever you want,
v of t is Gaussian.

00:42:14.980 --> 00:42:19.550
So then this is a regularizer
which if you read it,

00:42:19.550 --> 00:42:24.430
you will understand
completely is

00:42:24.430 --> 00:42:28.900
a sparsifier for differences
changes in a and b.

00:42:28.900 --> 00:42:30.280
Everybody got that?

00:42:30.280 --> 00:42:32.420
That's a sparsifier
for the difference,

00:42:32.420 --> 00:42:35.290
which is basically saying I
want a piecewise constant.

00:42:35.290 --> 00:42:38.290
So here's a quick example.

00:42:38.290 --> 00:42:40.525
So here's some data
that was generated.

00:42:40.525 --> 00:42:42.370
Here's a and b.

00:42:42.370 --> 00:42:43.150
It doesn't matter.

00:42:43.150 --> 00:42:45.040
But actually, since
you see a and b,

00:42:45.040 --> 00:42:47.950
you can see they change at t
equals 100 and t equals 200.

00:42:47.950 --> 00:42:51.320
And if you look at your
eyeball and draw a line here,

00:42:51.320 --> 00:42:53.830
you can look at that and look
at that and your eyeball,

00:42:53.830 --> 00:42:56.350
your spectral analyzer
in your eyeball

00:42:56.350 --> 00:42:58.390
says something happened.

00:42:58.390 --> 00:43:03.910
And then you can again, this
is hard, but you could pretend,

00:43:03.910 --> 00:43:05.920
especially after
you knew the truth,

00:43:05.920 --> 00:43:08.600
you could certainly
pretend that you'd say,

00:43:08.600 --> 00:43:11.120
oh, yeah, no, I can see that
the spectrum is different.

00:43:11.120 --> 00:43:13.660
In fact, the
autocorrelation changes.

00:43:13.660 --> 00:43:16.910
That's where people
who have the ability

00:43:16.910 --> 00:43:19.820
to look at a plot
of something and say

00:43:19.820 --> 00:43:21.980
what the autocorrelation
is in their head.

00:43:21.980 --> 00:43:23.630
So there are people like that.

00:43:23.630 --> 00:43:25.130
But anyway, so you
can see something

00:43:25.130 --> 00:43:26.338
happened in that three times.

00:43:26.338 --> 00:43:30.440
And the question is, from
the signal on the left,

00:43:30.440 --> 00:43:34.440
how would we guess these
changing coefficients?

00:43:34.440 --> 00:43:36.870
And here's what happens.

00:43:36.870 --> 00:43:38.820
If you just do
that l1 heuristic,

00:43:38.820 --> 00:43:41.190
you get this dashed line here.

00:43:41.190 --> 00:43:42.810
And it gets it.

00:43:42.810 --> 00:43:44.100
It's pretty good.

00:43:44.100 --> 00:43:46.380
You can see things like--

00:43:46.380 --> 00:43:49.830
it got pretty close to
where this change was.

00:43:49.830 --> 00:43:51.630
It was pretty good up there.

00:43:51.630 --> 00:43:54.550
But you see there's some
weird little things like that

00:43:54.550 --> 00:43:55.770
and this is sad.

00:43:55.770 --> 00:43:57.120
That's weird.

00:43:57.120 --> 00:43:58.950
If you do this
iterated l1 heuristic,

00:43:58.950 --> 00:44:01.090
you get that over there.

00:44:01.090 --> 00:44:04.620
That's actually pretty good.

00:44:04.620 --> 00:44:05.730
That's good.

00:44:05.730 --> 00:44:08.760
And actually, it's good.

00:44:08.760 --> 00:44:11.340
I swear I would have rerun this.

00:44:11.340 --> 00:44:14.250
If it turns out it had
nailed the actual times

00:44:14.250 --> 00:44:16.050
or the amplitudes
exactly, I would

00:44:16.050 --> 00:44:17.592
have rerun it because
that would have

00:44:17.592 --> 00:44:19.230
been sending the wrong message.

00:44:19.230 --> 00:44:21.100
But fortunately, it did not.

00:44:21.100 --> 00:44:21.600
It's fine.

00:44:21.600 --> 00:44:23.130
This is what you want to see.

00:44:23.130 --> 00:44:25.890
It does a pretty good
job of doing this.

00:44:25.890 --> 00:44:29.244
But, by the way, for vector--

00:44:29.244 --> 00:44:33.310
I'm sorry, for scalar AR
2 models, no big deal,

00:44:33.310 --> 00:44:34.420
your eyeball can do it.

00:44:34.420 --> 00:44:36.420
But what if I gave you
an econometric time

00:44:36.420 --> 00:44:40.420
series with 50 numbers?

00:44:40.420 --> 00:44:44.430
The dimension of 50 and I gave
it to you monthly for 50 years

00:44:44.430 --> 00:44:45.630
and you wanted to do this?

00:44:45.630 --> 00:44:52.370
Anyway, I guarantee your eyeball
could not do it, but you could.

00:44:52.370 --> 00:44:54.560
It's like 16 lines of Python.

00:44:54.560 --> 00:44:56.630
So I'm just saying.

00:44:56.630 --> 00:44:59.580
I'm showing you baby
examples, but trust me,

00:44:59.580 --> 00:45:03.858
these are the exact same methods
apply to much bigger and much

00:45:03.858 --> 00:45:04.900
more impressive examples.

00:45:09.030 --> 00:45:12.510
Last topic is
extension to matrices.

00:45:12.510 --> 00:45:14.770
This is actually very cool.

00:45:14.770 --> 00:45:19.810
So rank is a natural analog
of cardinality for matrices.

00:45:19.810 --> 00:45:22.890
There's lots of
ways to say that.

00:45:22.890 --> 00:45:24.928
And a convex-rank
problem is a problem

00:45:24.928 --> 00:45:26.970
that's convex, except
there are rank constraints.

00:45:26.970 --> 00:45:33.030
There's tons of these problems
that come up all the time.

00:45:33.030 --> 00:45:35.740
And there's lots of
connections to them.

00:45:35.740 --> 00:45:39.720
But it turns out the analog of
the l1 norm is extremely cool.

00:45:42.440 --> 00:45:45.420
This is the dual of
the spectral norm.

00:45:45.420 --> 00:45:49.470
The spectral norm is the
maximum of the singular values.

00:45:49.470 --> 00:45:51.450
The dual of that--

00:45:51.450 --> 00:45:53.940
surprise, surprise, it's the
sum of the singular values.

00:45:53.940 --> 00:45:56.343
By now, you should be seeing
some of these patterns.

00:45:56.343 --> 00:45:58.260
I don't know when you
study music or something

00:45:58.260 --> 00:46:01.410
and you start hearing,
you see themes and things

00:46:01.410 --> 00:46:03.370
like that, you should
start seeing them.

00:46:03.370 --> 00:46:06.450
So now you know the dual
of the spectral norm

00:46:06.450 --> 00:46:10.270
is the sum of the singular
values, which is cool.

00:46:10.270 --> 00:46:11.500
And guess what?

00:46:11.500 --> 00:46:15.640
It's a low rank.

00:46:15.640 --> 00:46:17.800
We don't have a verb
like sparsifying,

00:46:17.800 --> 00:46:19.900
but it's a low rankifying.

00:46:19.900 --> 00:46:20.650
That's not a verb.

00:46:20.650 --> 00:46:26.740
But if it were a verb, that's
a low-rankifying regularizer.

00:46:26.740 --> 00:46:29.287
So lots of cool stuff.

00:46:29.287 --> 00:46:30.370
I'll give you one example.

00:46:30.370 --> 00:46:34.030
And now this is research
from only 10 years ago.

00:46:34.030 --> 00:46:40.330
Suppose I give you some
samples from a Gaussian vector,

00:46:40.330 --> 00:46:42.790
a Gaussian, but I'm
going to give you

00:46:42.790 --> 00:46:46.330
the prior that the inverse of
the covariance matrix, which

00:46:46.330 --> 00:46:49.580
is the precision matrix is
sparse, which by the way,

00:46:49.580 --> 00:46:53.540
has a beautiful interpretation
because sparsity

00:46:53.540 --> 00:46:55.930
in the precision
matrix corresponds

00:46:55.930 --> 00:46:57.820
to conditional independence.

00:46:57.820 --> 00:47:00.310
So basically says,
here are these things.

00:47:00.310 --> 00:47:04.180
My prior is that it comes
from a sparse Bayesian model

00:47:04.180 --> 00:47:05.815
because that's
exactly what that is.

00:47:05.815 --> 00:47:08.190
And by the way, if you don't
know what I'm talking about,

00:47:08.190 --> 00:47:09.940
well, you should
probably learn this stuff

00:47:09.940 --> 00:47:11.207
because it's really cool.

00:47:11.207 --> 00:47:12.540
If you do then that's fine, too.

00:47:15.090 --> 00:47:17.880
Then you would know what to do.

00:47:17.880 --> 00:47:22.230
You maximize the log-likelihood
minus the dual norm

00:47:22.230 --> 00:47:25.530
of the precision matrix.

00:47:25.530 --> 00:47:29.640
And you would be about 8 to
10 years ago in research.

00:47:29.640 --> 00:47:32.730
So it's cool.

00:47:32.730 --> 00:47:36.570
You're very close to the
boundaries of research here.

00:47:36.570 --> 00:47:38.340
OK.

00:47:38.340 --> 00:47:42.720
I think maybe I'll even skip
factor modeling as an example

00:47:42.720 --> 00:47:46.090
and I'll quit there.

00:47:46.090 --> 00:47:50.250
And now we want to switch
to something else, which

00:47:50.250 --> 00:47:51.737
is going to be--

00:47:51.737 --> 00:47:55.215
oops, let's switch over to here.

00:47:57.980 --> 00:47:58.480
OK.

00:47:58.480 --> 00:48:01.030
So at this point,
the class is over.

00:48:01.030 --> 00:48:02.975
So we're done.

00:48:02.975 --> 00:48:04.225
I mean, not for you, actually.

00:48:04.225 --> 00:48:05.710
Actually, for that matter or us.

00:48:05.710 --> 00:48:10.663
But you'll finish your
homework 8 by tomorrow.

00:48:10.663 --> 00:48:12.580
Hopefully, you'll get a
working barrier method

00:48:12.580 --> 00:48:15.080
or something like that and then
you'll feel good about that.

00:48:15.080 --> 00:48:16.810
You should feel good about that.

00:48:16.810 --> 00:48:20.650
Then you have a week to poke
through all the material.

00:48:20.650 --> 00:48:22.240
You really should
go back for fun

00:48:22.240 --> 00:48:27.220
and read a couple of sections
in duality or convex functions

00:48:27.220 --> 00:48:30.160
because you've been living
with them for nine weeks.

00:48:30.160 --> 00:48:33.370
So it should be like the
first time you saw this

00:48:33.370 --> 00:48:35.800
and you thought log
determinant or something

00:48:35.800 --> 00:48:38.230
like that or the min
of the square root,

00:48:38.230 --> 00:48:39.650
it probably looked pretty weird.

00:48:39.650 --> 00:48:42.715
It should look very
familiar to you now, I hope.

00:48:45.970 --> 00:48:48.460
So you're going to have a
whole week of just letting

00:48:48.460 --> 00:48:52.630
all this stuff process and
all the neural links formed

00:48:52.630 --> 00:48:53.540
and stuff like that.

00:48:53.540 --> 00:48:56.123
So what I want to do now is just
actually talk about just some

00:48:56.123 --> 00:48:58.480
of the super high-level
main ideas in the class.

00:49:04.430 --> 00:49:07.130
So, first of all,
there's a ton of problems

00:49:07.130 --> 00:49:12.470
in any mathematical area
that reduced to optimization.

00:49:12.470 --> 00:49:16.610
So engineering design, data
analysis and statistics,

00:49:16.610 --> 00:49:19.370
economics, management,
all of these things,

00:49:19.370 --> 00:49:23.930
they're just
optimization problems.

00:49:23.930 --> 00:49:28.550
Frankly, that's a tautology.

00:49:28.550 --> 00:49:30.620
So, of course, they're
all optimization problems.

00:49:30.620 --> 00:49:32.390
That's not an
interesting statement.

00:49:32.390 --> 00:49:36.260
I mean, it's an interesting
way to think about things.

00:49:36.260 --> 00:49:38.330
I think it's actually
super clear way to do it.

00:49:38.330 --> 00:49:40.040
You go into a situation and
people are coming at you

00:49:40.040 --> 00:49:42.230
from four different ways
telling you, oh, there's this,

00:49:42.230 --> 00:49:43.880
and we have to do that,
and these constraints

00:49:43.880 --> 00:49:46.172
don't forget about, oh,
there's regulatory, blah, blah,

00:49:46.172 --> 00:49:47.150
and you're like, ah.

00:49:47.150 --> 00:49:49.640
And you just sit down and
you say, OK, wait, sorry.

00:49:49.640 --> 00:49:51.950
What are the things you're
allowed to mess with?

00:49:51.950 --> 00:49:53.930
These are the variables.

00:49:53.930 --> 00:49:58.853
What are the things
that are nonnegotiable?

00:49:58.853 --> 00:50:01.520
And they would say, well, these,
and those are your constraints.

00:50:01.520 --> 00:50:04.230
And then you'd say, what
would make you happy?

00:50:04.230 --> 00:50:07.510
Now they probably are going to
mention two or three things.

00:50:07.510 --> 00:50:09.780
And then you know you have
a multicriterion problem.

00:50:09.780 --> 00:50:12.545
And you'd say, cool,
I will scalarize it

00:50:12.545 --> 00:50:13.920
with a bunch of
weights and we'll

00:50:13.920 --> 00:50:16.290
play with those later to see
how happy we can make you.

00:50:16.290 --> 00:50:17.880
Everybody following
what I just said?

00:50:17.880 --> 00:50:20.100
So it's actually not a bad
way to organize your thoughts.

00:50:20.100 --> 00:50:20.970
When you get into
something there's

00:50:20.970 --> 00:50:22.845
people coming at you
from 50 different things

00:50:22.845 --> 00:50:27.525
and they say, oh, whatever,
remember rule 144B and remember

00:50:27.525 --> 00:50:31.110
the Basel requirements for
capital and you're like, wah.

00:50:31.110 --> 00:50:32.700
Anyway, so it's
just a very good way

00:50:32.700 --> 00:50:34.645
to actually just do anything.

00:50:34.645 --> 00:50:36.270
Not just there, but
actually any field.

00:50:39.900 --> 00:50:44.220
So basically, tractability.

00:50:44.220 --> 00:50:45.960
Roughly speaking,
but some people

00:50:45.960 --> 00:50:47.520
get really weird about this.

00:50:47.520 --> 00:50:48.360
Tractability.

00:50:48.360 --> 00:50:52.890
The problems that are tractable
are the ones which are convex.

00:50:52.890 --> 00:50:56.640
And there's people that get
weird and extreme about this.

00:50:56.640 --> 00:50:58.110
I'm not one of them.

00:50:58.110 --> 00:51:01.350
So they would actually go
through the undergrad CS course

00:51:01.350 --> 00:51:04.620
and they'd say, ha, you can find
the shortest path in a graph

00:51:04.620 --> 00:51:05.520
from here to here.

00:51:05.520 --> 00:51:06.703
That's polynomial time.

00:51:06.703 --> 00:51:07.870
That's not a convex problem.

00:51:07.870 --> 00:51:09.390
Well, the answer is yes, it is.

00:51:09.390 --> 00:51:12.480
It turns out it's one where
the relaxation has zero duality

00:51:12.480 --> 00:51:13.680
gap.

00:51:13.680 --> 00:51:16.320
Or they say, I can take the
singular value decomposition.

00:51:16.320 --> 00:51:17.250
That's not convex.

00:51:17.250 --> 00:51:18.480
And then it's even weirder.

00:51:18.480 --> 00:51:22.050
They would say, oh,
it is because that's

00:51:22.050 --> 00:51:23.843
a non-convex problem
with zero duality gap.

00:51:23.843 --> 00:51:25.260
This is getting
weird because it's

00:51:25.260 --> 00:51:29.120
just we know how to do that.

00:51:29.120 --> 00:51:31.100
And, of course,
the other thing is

00:51:31.100 --> 00:51:33.530
we shouldn't get too bent
out of shape about it

00:51:33.530 --> 00:51:37.880
because in 95% of
applications when you're

00:51:37.880 --> 00:51:39.610
solving an optimization
problem, that's

00:51:39.610 --> 00:51:41.360
just a surrogate for
what you really want.

00:51:41.360 --> 00:51:45.770
And that's actually something
good to keep in mind.

00:51:45.770 --> 00:51:48.190
So if you're doing
machine learning,

00:51:48.190 --> 00:51:51.400
you don't care about minimizing
a loss plus a regularizer.

00:51:51.400 --> 00:51:54.520
You want to have good
predictions on data

00:51:54.520 --> 00:51:57.550
you've never seen before.

00:51:57.550 --> 00:51:59.680
If you're making
a drone trajectory

00:51:59.680 --> 00:52:02.260
and you're running MPC
or something like that,

00:52:02.260 --> 00:52:05.140
you don't care about solving
whatever optimization problem

00:52:05.140 --> 00:52:08.560
of somebody, you want it to
follow the trajectory well

00:52:08.560 --> 00:52:12.340
and use as little fuel or, in
that case, energy as possible.

00:52:12.340 --> 00:52:13.900
Everybody see what I'm saying?

00:52:13.900 --> 00:52:15.940
If you're doing
finance, you just

00:52:15.940 --> 00:52:18.550
want to make money
without taking risks.

00:52:18.550 --> 00:52:22.060
If someone chooses to solve
some silly convex optimization

00:52:22.060 --> 00:52:26.710
problem to do it, good for
them, but you're not paid.

00:52:26.710 --> 00:52:30.095
We should just all bear in mind
that in almost all applications

00:52:30.095 --> 00:52:31.720
the optimization
problem you're solving

00:52:31.720 --> 00:52:34.420
is actually just a surrogate
for what you really want.

00:52:34.420 --> 00:52:37.570
So that's an important
thing to remember.

00:52:37.570 --> 00:52:41.060
And when you think about
that, then you realize,

00:52:41.060 --> 00:52:46.440
it's not that big a deal if you
can't solve a problem globally.

00:52:46.440 --> 00:52:48.620
It's perfectly cool if you can.

00:52:48.620 --> 00:52:53.480
But if you can't, it's
just not that big a deal.

00:52:53.480 --> 00:52:56.870
So if someone took
your circuit design

00:52:56.870 --> 00:53:01.010
and reduced the dynamic
power consumption by 20%,

00:53:01.010 --> 00:53:02.840
you're happy.

00:53:02.840 --> 00:53:05.030
Maybe you think could
someone do it by 30,

00:53:05.030 --> 00:53:08.430
but you're just happy you
just reduced it to 25%.

00:53:08.430 --> 00:53:11.570
So I'm just saying we shouldn't
get too hung up on this.

00:53:11.570 --> 00:53:14.600
Nevertheless, tractable
is not a bad thing.

00:53:14.600 --> 00:53:18.938
[LAUGHS] So to be able to say, I
can solve this problem exactly,

00:53:18.938 --> 00:53:19.980
there's a lot of benefit.

00:53:19.980 --> 00:53:22.220
I mean, one benefit is
there's no babysitting

00:53:22.220 --> 00:53:24.440
of the algorithm.

00:53:24.440 --> 00:53:28.250
When you close your eyes
and think of any place

00:53:28.250 --> 00:53:31.190
where people widely used
non-convex optimization,

00:53:31.190 --> 00:53:34.040
anyone here can think
of a bunch of those.

00:53:34.040 --> 00:53:36.350
It turns out actually
like quote solving

00:53:36.350 --> 00:53:38.360
those problems which
you're not actually doing

00:53:38.360 --> 00:53:39.690
is actually an art.

00:53:39.690 --> 00:53:41.658
And somebody says,
oh, no, no, you

00:53:41.658 --> 00:53:43.200
should be using this
and that and you

00:53:43.200 --> 00:53:45.540
should change the learning
rate and blah, blah, blah.

00:53:45.540 --> 00:53:47.400
And that's just all
short-circuited.

00:53:47.400 --> 00:53:48.703
You just don't have it.

00:53:48.703 --> 00:53:50.370
Or what's your initial--
like you asked,

00:53:50.370 --> 00:53:52.710
what's the initial condition?

00:53:52.710 --> 00:53:54.210
That's a pretty
good circuit design,

00:53:54.210 --> 00:53:55.957
but what was your
initial design?

00:53:55.957 --> 00:53:58.290
And the point about this is
that's just short-circuited.

00:53:58.290 --> 00:53:59.207
There's no such thing.

00:53:59.207 --> 00:54:00.630
You don't have any of that.

00:54:00.630 --> 00:54:02.670
It's just all gone.

00:54:02.670 --> 00:54:04.860
The other advantage
of course being

00:54:04.860 --> 00:54:07.950
the fact that they can be
made unbelievably reliable

00:54:07.950 --> 00:54:09.940
and therefore they
can be embedded.

00:54:09.940 --> 00:54:12.930
So they are embedded
in control systems

00:54:12.930 --> 00:54:16.990
where they solve
1,000 QPs per second.

00:54:16.990 --> 00:54:20.210
And they do that for hours and
hours and days and days, just

00:54:20.210 --> 00:54:20.710
forever.

00:54:20.710 --> 00:54:22.480
And it just works.

00:54:22.480 --> 00:54:26.320
And try doing that
with a non-convex thing

00:54:26.320 --> 00:54:28.630
because it's not going to
take long before something

00:54:28.630 --> 00:54:31.840
fails to converge or
something like that.

00:54:31.840 --> 00:54:34.060
OK.

00:54:34.060 --> 00:54:35.740
So it turns out a
lot of applications

00:54:35.740 --> 00:54:37.948
that you've seen in this
class they can be formulated

00:54:37.948 --> 00:54:38.860
as convex problems.

00:54:38.860 --> 00:54:40.390
We go a little bit
overboard on it

00:54:40.390 --> 00:54:42.280
because you're in a
little sandbox now.

00:54:42.280 --> 00:54:47.180
But once you finish the
course, you can venture out.

00:54:47.180 --> 00:54:50.170
So it turns out most problems
aren't really convex.

00:54:50.170 --> 00:54:52.120
But it turns out once
you know these tools,

00:54:52.120 --> 00:54:54.880
you're going to be unbelievably
effective at problems

00:54:54.880 --> 00:54:56.050
that aren't convex.

00:54:56.050 --> 00:54:59.530
if someone will say, I
want to do a trade list.

00:54:59.530 --> 00:55:02.250
You say, oh, but there's a
minimum non-zero trade size.

00:55:02.250 --> 00:55:04.600
I'm sorry, don't come
back and tell me to buy

00:55:04.600 --> 00:55:06.220
$300 worth of something.

00:55:06.220 --> 00:55:07.990
Minimum is $10,000.

00:55:07.990 --> 00:55:09.300
Everybody see what I'm saying?

00:55:09.300 --> 00:55:12.840
It's either 0 or a
minimum of 10,000.

00:55:12.840 --> 00:55:15.700
That actually
happens all the time.

00:55:15.700 --> 00:55:19.500
But you know these things.

00:55:19.500 --> 00:55:21.500
All of you could figure
out a heuristic for that

00:55:21.500 --> 00:55:24.170
and it would be extremely good.

00:55:24.170 --> 00:55:25.595
So, in fact,
somebody just come up

00:55:25.595 --> 00:55:29.565
with a heuristic
for that right now.

00:55:29.565 --> 00:55:30.440
Let's make it simple.

00:55:30.440 --> 00:55:31.898
I'm going to sell
a bunch of stuff.

00:55:31.898 --> 00:55:34.580
I have 6,000 assets,
I'm going to sell some.

00:55:34.580 --> 00:55:35.900
But here are the rules.

00:55:35.900 --> 00:55:38.180
Don't ask me to sell
$300 worth of something.

00:55:38.180 --> 00:55:39.710
That's insulting.

00:55:39.710 --> 00:55:42.290
Make it something--
it's either you don't

00:55:42.290 --> 00:55:43.967
sell any or it's $10,000 worth.

00:55:43.967 --> 00:55:45.800
Somebody come up with
a heuristic right now.

00:55:45.800 --> 00:55:47.300
And the rest of
the problem is I've

00:55:47.300 --> 00:55:49.938
got transaction cost,
risk, blah, blah, blah.

00:55:49.938 --> 00:55:50.480
I don't know.

00:55:50.480 --> 00:55:52.007
So somebody give me some--

00:55:52.007 --> 00:55:53.090
well, how would you do it?

00:55:56.550 --> 00:55:59.580
I say you could do it right now.

00:55:59.580 --> 00:56:02.100
Well, maybe after
some reflection.

00:56:02.100 --> 00:56:04.195
What would you do?

00:56:04.195 --> 00:56:07.837
What's the first thing you
would do in that situation?

00:56:07.837 --> 00:56:09.420
Let me describe it
in a different way.

00:56:09.420 --> 00:56:11.850
Somebody comes into you and
they're ranting and raving

00:56:11.850 --> 00:56:14.110
and they say, here's
what I want you to do.

00:56:14.110 --> 00:56:17.670
And they list off 12
constraints and objectives which

00:56:17.670 --> 00:56:21.377
are convex and one that's not.

00:56:21.377 --> 00:56:22.710
Do you have a suggestion for me?

00:56:25.258 --> 00:56:27.050
The street fighting
suggestion, what is it?

00:56:27.050 --> 00:56:28.310
What would you do?

00:56:28.310 --> 00:56:29.150
Relax the last one.

00:56:29.150 --> 00:56:30.270
Yeah, relax the last one.

00:56:30.270 --> 00:56:31.190
Which means?

00:56:31.190 --> 00:56:33.152
Let's be brutally honest.

00:56:33.152 --> 00:56:34.885
[INAUDIBLE]

00:56:34.885 --> 00:56:35.510
How about this?

00:56:35.510 --> 00:56:36.830
How about just ignore it?

00:56:36.830 --> 00:56:37.710
[LAUGHTER]

00:56:39.230 --> 00:56:41.700
That's a strong
form of relaxation.

00:56:41.700 --> 00:56:45.215
So you solve it without it.

00:56:45.215 --> 00:56:47.090
You can put an l1 norm
in there, that's fine.

00:56:47.090 --> 00:56:49.580
Let's put an l1 norm
in there and then

00:56:49.580 --> 00:56:53.630
you look at all your trades,
and then what do you do?

00:56:53.630 --> 00:56:57.020
If they come out above
$10,000, they're fine.

00:56:57.020 --> 00:56:58.978
If they're 0, they're fine.

00:56:58.978 --> 00:57:01.020
If they're in that weird
dead band in the middle,

00:57:01.020 --> 00:57:03.562
they're not cool according to
your trading desk or something.

00:57:03.562 --> 00:57:05.390
Everybody following this?

00:57:05.390 --> 00:57:07.910
I could transpose this
to any field there is.

00:57:07.910 --> 00:57:11.060
I guarantee you, exact
identical things happen.

00:57:11.060 --> 00:57:14.810
Here's one, a generator is
either off and generating 0

00:57:14.810 --> 00:57:18.900
power, or the minimum
is 10 megawatts.

00:57:18.900 --> 00:57:22.320
So it's 0 or between
10 and 30 megawatts.

00:57:22.320 --> 00:57:23.250
End of story.

00:57:23.250 --> 00:57:25.330
That's identical to this.

00:57:25.330 --> 00:57:25.830
All right.

00:57:25.830 --> 00:57:28.170
So what you do now
is anybody-- what

00:57:28.170 --> 00:57:31.530
you do is you solve it once
then you simply have a threshold

00:57:31.530 --> 00:57:35.100
and you decide which generator
is on and which is off.

00:57:35.100 --> 00:57:36.900
Now you resolve with
that as a constraint.

00:57:36.900 --> 00:57:39.360
If you're on, you're bigger
than your minimum power.

00:57:39.360 --> 00:57:40.590
Let's make it 10 megawatts.

00:57:40.590 --> 00:57:43.530
If you're trading you'd
say I'm going to sell more.

00:57:43.530 --> 00:57:46.050
Anybody that came if you're on--

00:57:46.050 --> 00:57:47.880
I'm actually selling
you, I have to sell

00:57:47.880 --> 00:57:49.050
more than $10,000 worth.

00:57:49.050 --> 00:57:49.988
Everybody got this?

00:57:49.988 --> 00:57:51.030
And everything else is 0.

00:57:51.030 --> 00:57:52.650
That's two convex problems.

00:57:52.650 --> 00:57:54.880
This would work beautifully.

00:57:54.880 --> 00:57:56.160
It's heuristic.

00:57:56.160 --> 00:58:00.540
This is just an example
to say that we do stuff

00:58:00.540 --> 00:58:01.860
that's mostly just convex.

00:58:01.860 --> 00:58:03.810
But once you absorb
all these tools,

00:58:03.810 --> 00:58:07.385
you will be extremely
effective at things like that.

00:58:12.250 --> 00:58:12.750
All right.

00:58:12.750 --> 00:58:15.600
So the theoretical
consequences of convexity

00:58:15.600 --> 00:58:19.380
are local optima or global.

00:58:19.380 --> 00:58:21.870
Duality theory is awesome.

00:58:21.870 --> 00:58:25.530
Basically, I think of it as
a completely systematic way

00:58:25.530 --> 00:58:29.670
to derive sophisticated lower
bounds on the optimal value

00:58:29.670 --> 00:58:32.550
of a problem, which is cool.

00:58:32.550 --> 00:58:36.630
In the case of
convexity, they become

00:58:36.630 --> 00:58:37.770
necessary and sufficient.

00:58:37.770 --> 00:58:38.730
I mean, not quite.

00:58:38.730 --> 00:58:39.970
There's a little asterisk.

00:58:39.970 --> 00:58:43.560
There's some constraint
qualification, but that's fine.

00:58:43.560 --> 00:58:45.150
And that's super useful.

00:58:45.150 --> 00:58:48.630
It allows you to do things
sensitivity analysis.

00:58:48.630 --> 00:58:51.960
It produces things like
infeasibility certificates.

00:58:51.960 --> 00:58:55.660
When you come back and you go, I
can't do it, I can't design it.

00:58:55.660 --> 00:58:56.940
Someone says, why not?

00:58:56.940 --> 00:58:57.990
And you go, no one can.

00:58:57.990 --> 00:58:59.883
And they go, well,
you can't, but I'll

00:58:59.883 --> 00:59:01.800
fire you and your group
and I'll get a new one

00:59:01.800 --> 00:59:03.092
and they will be able to maybe.

00:59:03.092 --> 00:59:05.893
And you go, no, because I
can actually easily prove

00:59:05.893 --> 00:59:06.810
that no one can do it.

00:59:06.810 --> 00:59:07.643
Everybody follow me?

00:59:07.643 --> 00:59:10.140
That's a certificate
of infeasibility.

00:59:10.140 --> 00:59:11.580
So that's cool.

00:59:14.100 --> 00:59:17.250
If you care about the worst-case
complexity theory then

00:59:17.250 --> 00:59:18.870
there is one.

00:59:18.870 --> 00:59:21.160
So that's fine.

00:59:21.160 --> 00:59:25.570
The practical ones are actually
I think much more interesting.

00:59:25.570 --> 00:59:30.790
So basically, if a problem
is convex, you can solve it.

00:59:30.790 --> 00:59:37.090
I'm aware of no situation
where someone proposed a convex

00:59:37.090 --> 00:59:41.920
problem that they
couldn't end up--

00:59:41.920 --> 00:59:45.160
not all of them are
going to work with CVXPY,

00:59:45.160 --> 00:59:48.190
but people in machine learning
they were like, excuse me,

00:59:48.190 --> 00:59:50.145
I have a billion samples.

00:59:50.145 --> 00:59:51.770
And by the way, 100
million parameters.

00:59:51.770 --> 00:59:53.865
So it's not working.

00:59:53.865 --> 00:59:54.740
People figure it out.

00:59:54.740 --> 00:59:56.560
In that case, you use
these other methods

00:59:56.560 --> 01:00:00.110
like subgradient methods
or something like that.

01:00:00.110 --> 01:00:01.900
People for images.

01:00:01.900 --> 01:00:03.975
And you can do a lot
of that stuff, too.

01:00:03.975 --> 01:00:06.470
It's all fine.

01:00:06.470 --> 01:00:10.490
So anyway, this
is the basic idea.

01:00:10.490 --> 01:00:13.760
But for normal-sized
problems, if you're not

01:00:13.760 --> 01:00:18.680
doing images or giant things
or handling video or something

01:00:18.680 --> 01:00:24.560
like that, then you're within
striking distance of these kind

01:00:24.560 --> 01:00:26.840
of easy-to-use tools.

01:00:26.840 --> 01:00:32.090
So the implications of things
like CVXPY are immense.

01:00:32.090 --> 01:00:35.090
And actually, I can
tell you specifically

01:00:35.090 --> 01:00:37.760
why you should be extremely
grateful that you're

01:00:37.760 --> 01:00:40.440
taking this in a
post-CVXPY world

01:00:40.440 --> 01:00:42.440
because I'll tell you how
it worked before that.

01:00:42.440 --> 01:00:43.850
Here's the way it worked.

01:00:43.850 --> 01:00:45.320
I'd go into my
office, there'll be

01:00:45.320 --> 01:00:47.660
a bunch of grad students
there, and I'd say,

01:00:47.660 --> 01:00:50.150
I've got this
unbelievably good idea.

01:00:50.150 --> 01:00:53.130
Here's how we're going to--
we're solve this convex problem

01:00:53.130 --> 01:00:59.480
and it's going to work really
well for some application.

01:00:59.480 --> 01:01:00.600
And they go, cool.

01:01:00.600 --> 01:01:01.850
Then here's what would happen.

01:01:01.850 --> 01:01:06.270
I would go home, have a nice
dinner, that kind of thing.

01:01:06.270 --> 01:01:08.760
Grad students would sit
around and implement some--

01:01:08.760 --> 01:01:11.040
and it was not--
it's not E, I mean,

01:01:11.040 --> 01:01:14.130
they would have MOSEK or some
commercial solver, who knows,

01:01:14.130 --> 01:01:15.090
or some other one.

01:01:15.090 --> 01:01:19.140
They'd be writing C++ and
basically stuffing matrices

01:01:19.140 --> 01:01:20.130
by themselves.

01:01:20.130 --> 01:01:22.140
Everyone following this?

01:01:22.140 --> 01:01:23.670
It would not be fun.

01:01:23.670 --> 01:01:26.170
I would come in the next
morning all well-rested

01:01:26.170 --> 01:01:27.420
and I'd say, hey, how'd it go?

01:01:27.420 --> 01:01:28.962
And they'd go, oh,
we implemented it.

01:01:28.962 --> 01:01:29.838
And I go, cool.

01:01:29.838 --> 01:01:30.630
How's does it work?

01:01:30.630 --> 01:01:31.470
And they go, not too well.

01:01:31.470 --> 01:01:33.450
And I go, oh, well, I
got another great idea.

01:01:33.450 --> 01:01:34.510
[LAUGHTER]

01:01:36.220 --> 01:01:38.200
So I think actually,
it's basically

01:01:38.200 --> 01:01:41.080
grad students who should be
most grateful for the existence

01:01:41.080 --> 01:01:44.980
of these high-level tools
because they're the,

01:01:44.980 --> 01:01:48.920
I believe, the
chief beneficiaries.

01:01:48.920 --> 01:01:49.420
OK.

01:01:49.420 --> 01:01:52.100
So how do you use it?

01:01:52.100 --> 01:01:53.860
People have different
ideas about this,

01:01:53.860 --> 01:01:56.710
but these are just mine.

01:01:56.710 --> 01:02:01.660
Basically, I'd start with
this rapid prototyping.

01:02:01.660 --> 01:02:06.490
So you start with really
small problem instances

01:02:06.490 --> 01:02:08.860
and you can use some
inefficient method.

01:02:08.860 --> 01:02:09.995
It doesn't matter.

01:02:09.995 --> 01:02:12.370
By the way, if you don't like
the results there, then you

01:02:12.370 --> 01:02:13.980
just--

01:02:13.980 --> 01:02:16.570
if someone else is saying like,
it's never going to scale,

01:02:16.570 --> 01:02:18.670
it's never going to
scale, what are you doing?

01:02:18.670 --> 01:02:20.650
If you don't like what
it does with problems

01:02:20.650 --> 01:02:23.048
with 1,000 or 10,000
variables, then why on Earth

01:02:23.048 --> 01:02:25.090
would you think you're
going to like what it does

01:02:25.090 --> 01:02:27.550
for 1 million or 100 million?

01:02:27.550 --> 01:02:29.530
By the way, there's
some people--

01:02:29.530 --> 01:02:31.420
I got yelled at by
someone at Google who

01:02:31.420 --> 01:02:33.740
once said, "That's so wrong."

01:02:33.740 --> 01:02:37.400
He said, "The things we work
on, the interesting things

01:02:37.400 --> 01:02:39.410
emerge at scale."

01:02:39.410 --> 01:02:41.960
And that's supposed to sound
really deep and all that.

01:02:41.960 --> 01:02:43.040
And it's true.

01:02:43.040 --> 01:02:43.970
But it's fine.

01:02:43.970 --> 01:02:46.475
And they're like, no,
we implement at scale.

01:02:46.475 --> 01:02:47.850
And I'm like,
well, good for you.

01:02:47.850 --> 01:02:49.790
So I'm just saying there's
diversity of opinions

01:02:49.790 --> 01:02:50.665
about how to do this.

01:02:50.665 --> 01:02:54.830
But, anyway, you heard mine,
and there's others who say that.

01:02:58.070 --> 01:03:00.560
So you can always do cool stuff.

01:03:00.560 --> 01:03:03.080
You can work out and simplify
the optimality conditions

01:03:03.080 --> 01:03:04.550
and dual.

01:03:04.550 --> 01:03:07.460
Honestly, in most cases,
that doesn't actually

01:03:07.460 --> 01:03:10.010
have any practical
use because you're

01:03:10.010 --> 01:03:11.340
going to solve it and stuff.

01:03:11.340 --> 01:03:13.770
So you don't need
to worry about that.

01:03:13.770 --> 01:03:16.940
And in some cases, there
are some rare cases,

01:03:16.940 --> 01:03:19.160
but it's actually
interesting because it's

01:03:19.160 --> 01:03:20.690
cool to say, hey, guess what?

01:03:20.690 --> 01:03:22.760
We get prices automatically.

01:03:22.760 --> 01:03:26.720
We get energy prices
in 15-minute intervals

01:03:26.720 --> 01:03:28.370
at all these different nodes.

01:03:28.370 --> 01:03:29.150
There you go.

01:03:29.150 --> 01:03:32.870
This is $87 per megawatt
hour from 2:00 to 2:15 PM

01:03:32.870 --> 01:03:35.080
at this node.

01:03:35.080 --> 01:03:37.060
So there are cases
where it's actually

01:03:37.060 --> 01:03:39.730
really useful to understand
the dual and work it out.

01:03:39.730 --> 01:03:41.710
In other cases, though,
it's mostly there

01:03:41.710 --> 01:03:43.900
to interpret your--

01:03:43.900 --> 01:03:46.870
I mean, actually, to
impress your friends, then

01:03:46.870 --> 01:03:48.040
you can sound really--

01:03:48.040 --> 01:03:48.970
you don't really care.

01:03:48.970 --> 01:03:51.290
You would say like, I'm
designing this thing

01:03:51.290 --> 01:03:53.290
and you'd say what the
dual is, and they go, oh,

01:03:53.290 --> 01:03:53.900
that's awesome.

01:03:53.900 --> 01:03:54.220
That's cool.

01:03:54.220 --> 01:03:55.120
Did you use that to solve it?

01:03:55.120 --> 01:03:56.110
And you're like, no.

01:03:56.110 --> 01:03:59.260
But, anyway.

01:03:59.260 --> 01:04:03.850
Now, so it turns out once you
understand all the convex stuff

01:04:03.850 --> 01:04:06.730
and are pretty
fluent with it, you

01:04:06.730 --> 01:04:09.160
can do all sorts of crazy
stuff by repeatedly forming

01:04:09.160 --> 01:04:11.230
and solving a convex
approximation.

01:04:11.230 --> 01:04:13.450
We actually just
did that over here.

01:04:13.450 --> 01:04:18.070
So these are just super
good methods to know about.

01:04:18.070 --> 01:04:20.950
And there's a lot of
street fighting tricks.

01:04:20.950 --> 01:04:24.760
And someday I'm actually going
to put maybe a lecture together

01:04:24.760 --> 01:04:26.440
on street fighting tricks.

01:04:26.440 --> 01:04:28.930
I'd had everyone
sign an NDA saying

01:04:28.930 --> 01:04:31.150
that under no
circumstances could

01:04:31.150 --> 01:04:33.250
they divulge where they
learned these things

01:04:33.250 --> 01:04:36.170
and that I would deny it
if they pointed at me.

01:04:36.170 --> 01:04:39.635
Anyway, so I haven't done that
yet, but that's really cool.

01:04:39.635 --> 01:04:41.570
I just give you one for fun.

01:04:41.570 --> 01:04:42.920
Here's an example.

01:04:42.920 --> 01:04:45.260
You go on an internship
and someone says,

01:04:45.260 --> 01:04:47.103
generate me minimum
fuel trajectories

01:04:47.103 --> 01:04:48.770
that move this thing
from here to there.

01:04:48.770 --> 01:04:50.270
And you go, cool.

01:04:50.270 --> 01:04:52.255
Please tell me
about the fuel use.

01:04:52.255 --> 01:04:53.630
And they go, oh,
yeah, no problem

01:04:53.630 --> 01:04:54.890
because an intern last year.

01:04:54.890 --> 01:04:58.940
And then they dump something
this thick on your desk

01:04:58.940 --> 01:05:00.140
or whatever it is.

01:05:00.140 --> 01:05:01.550
And they go, yeah, that's it.

01:05:01.550 --> 01:05:04.910
And then they start talking
about thermodynamics and n1

01:05:04.910 --> 01:05:08.070
and n2 and these turbines
and their reflux in the past

01:05:08.070 --> 01:05:09.950
and you're like, bah.

01:05:09.950 --> 01:05:13.840
Anyway, then you say, do
you have a simulator for it?

01:05:13.840 --> 01:05:15.790
And they go, yeah, we do.

01:05:15.790 --> 01:05:19.690
Then you quiet, set up
a script to simulate it.

01:05:19.690 --> 01:05:23.290
And you find out that
the fuel use looks

01:05:23.290 --> 01:05:28.105
like this when you ask for--

01:05:28.105 --> 01:05:30.970
this is the thrust
you're requesting.

01:05:30.970 --> 01:05:33.658
And this is at idling and it
looks something like this.

01:05:33.658 --> 01:05:35.200
It goes like that,
then it's actually

01:05:35.200 --> 01:05:37.450
something weird like that,
and then it goes like that,

01:05:37.450 --> 01:05:39.160
and then it goes up like that.

01:05:39.160 --> 01:05:40.600
Everybody got this?

01:05:40.600 --> 01:05:42.890
And then someone who knows
the details would say,

01:05:42.890 --> 01:05:44.680
well, I know why
because when you get up

01:05:44.680 --> 01:05:46.660
to this amount of thrust
they open these valves

01:05:46.660 --> 01:05:47.410
and there's some--

01:05:47.410 --> 01:05:49.327
I'm just making this up,
there's some gas that

01:05:49.327 --> 01:05:51.130
flows over these four turbines.

01:05:51.130 --> 01:05:52.160
I don't know.

01:05:52.160 --> 01:05:53.830
Anyway, but it would
look like that.

01:05:53.830 --> 01:05:56.770
This would be the-- this
you got from a simulator.

01:05:56.770 --> 01:06:00.400
Everybody following this?

01:06:00.400 --> 01:06:02.260
What would you do
now that now we're

01:06:02.260 --> 01:06:05.440
in full street-fighting mode?

01:06:05.440 --> 01:06:06.290
What would you do?

01:06:06.290 --> 01:06:10.240
Well, first thing you do is
you'd say, it's not a convex.

01:06:10.240 --> 01:06:11.918
I want a suggestion.

01:06:11.918 --> 01:06:13.930
Get a convex surrogate?

01:06:13.930 --> 01:06:14.430
Yeah.

01:06:14.430 --> 01:06:15.570
Get a convex surrogate.

01:06:15.570 --> 01:06:19.335
Do you want me to
make one for you?

01:06:19.335 --> 01:06:20.460
Here's my convex surrogate.

01:06:20.460 --> 01:06:20.960
Ready?

01:06:20.960 --> 01:06:23.220
You just go like
that or something.

01:06:23.220 --> 01:06:25.200
It's fine.

01:06:25.200 --> 01:06:28.170
Now you solve the
problem quickly.

01:06:28.170 --> 01:06:30.450
You could even fit
this either by eyeball.

01:06:30.450 --> 01:06:33.008
If it's in one dimension,
you do it by eyeball.

01:06:33.008 --> 01:06:34.800
And then you just solve
it with this thing.

01:06:34.800 --> 01:06:36.840
It's fine.

01:06:36.840 --> 01:06:39.240
So everybody following this?

01:06:39.240 --> 01:06:42.810
So I'm just saying
that's not now,

01:06:42.810 --> 01:06:46.080
not until you get your
actual stamped certificate

01:06:46.080 --> 01:06:51.240
saying that you're now no
longer a trainee or something.

01:06:51.240 --> 01:06:53.040
But when you get
that certificate,

01:06:53.040 --> 01:06:55.990
you'll be allowed to-- so don't
do this for the next 10 days.

01:06:55.990 --> 01:07:00.030
But after that, you have a
license to do stuff like this.

01:07:00.030 --> 01:07:02.640
Everybody following this?

01:07:02.640 --> 01:07:05.052
OK.

01:07:05.052 --> 01:07:07.010
So there's a whole lot
of stuff we didn't cover

01:07:07.010 --> 01:07:08.593
and that's actually
that's something I

01:07:08.593 --> 01:07:11.670
want to talk about what's next.

01:07:11.670 --> 01:07:13.880
So we didn't talk
about this whole area

01:07:13.880 --> 01:07:16.233
of subgradient methods,
stochastic subgradient methods.

01:07:16.233 --> 01:07:18.650
And you've probably seen this
in a machine learning course

01:07:18.650 --> 01:07:20.990
or statistics course,
I would imagine.

01:07:20.990 --> 01:07:23.730
And it's absolutely fascinating.

01:07:23.730 --> 01:07:26.390
So a lot of stuff there.

01:07:26.390 --> 01:07:29.540
Actually, all of these
are taught in, I guess,

01:07:29.540 --> 01:07:31.010
several courses.

01:07:31.010 --> 01:07:34.470
And that's the next thing
we're going to talk about.

01:07:34.470 --> 01:07:36.413
So there's 364B.

01:07:36.413 --> 01:07:38.330
I think I actually have
a list of courses here

01:07:38.330 --> 01:07:39.710
but it's way out of date.

01:07:39.710 --> 01:07:42.380
So this is completely and
hideously out of date,

01:07:42.380 --> 01:07:43.392
but that's fine.

01:07:43.392 --> 01:07:44.600
I'll leave it up here anyway.

01:07:44.600 --> 01:07:47.150
So you have a couple of--

01:07:47.150 --> 01:07:52.010
you may never want to take
a course in this area again.

01:07:52.010 --> 01:07:58.160
But if you did, I'll
point you to some places.

01:07:58.160 --> 01:08:04.250
So one is 364B, which is
we cover a lot of material

01:08:04.250 --> 01:08:05.820
in that that's super useful.

01:08:05.820 --> 01:08:09.920
It's not as linear in its
dependency graph as this class.

01:08:09.920 --> 01:08:12.507
This class is you do
this, then you do that,

01:08:12.507 --> 01:08:14.090
then you do that,
then everything it's

01:08:14.090 --> 01:08:15.050
just very linear.

01:08:15.050 --> 01:08:17.240
That one is once you
know about subgradients,

01:08:17.240 --> 01:08:19.279
then the dependency
graph gets wide.

01:08:19.279 --> 01:08:21.149
And there's a lot of
choices of topics,

01:08:21.149 --> 01:08:23.120
but they're just
super good things.

01:08:23.120 --> 01:08:25.130
Actually, for
implementing-- I mean,

01:08:25.130 --> 01:08:27.590
some of the best things
you would learn in 364B

01:08:27.590 --> 01:08:34.819
would be how to solve relatively
big problems that would either

01:08:34.819 --> 01:08:38.600
take way too long using
CVXPY or just not even work.

01:08:38.600 --> 01:08:41.344
So if you were doing
image reconstruction

01:08:41.344 --> 01:08:45.229
or if you're reproducing
stuff from video data,

01:08:45.229 --> 01:08:46.010
it's just too big.

01:08:46.010 --> 01:08:49.370
I'm sorry, you can't plug
that in and stuff like that.

01:08:49.370 --> 01:08:50.750
You'd learn how to do that.

01:08:50.750 --> 01:08:53.083
You'd probably write it in
Torch or something like that,

01:08:53.083 --> 01:08:56.990
but you'd learn very effective,
simple methods for that.

01:08:56.990 --> 01:08:57.920
So that's fine.

01:08:57.920 --> 01:09:00.560
You'll learn about cool
things like distributed convex

01:09:00.560 --> 01:09:04.340
optimization, which
is super cool.

01:09:04.340 --> 01:09:06.229
I'll tell you how that works.

01:09:06.229 --> 01:09:07.700
You're sitting on
some data, you're

01:09:07.700 --> 01:09:09.408
sitting on some data,
and your hospitals,

01:09:09.408 --> 01:09:10.609
and you have some data, too.

01:09:10.609 --> 01:09:13.609
And I want to fit a model.

01:09:13.609 --> 01:09:18.325
I don't care a logistic
regression model of something.

01:09:18.325 --> 01:09:19.700
What's the likelihood
this person

01:09:19.700 --> 01:09:20.825
is going to develop sepsis?

01:09:20.825 --> 01:09:21.490
There we go.

01:09:21.490 --> 01:09:23.740
So you have a bunch of data,
you have a bunch of data,

01:09:23.740 --> 01:09:24.782
you have a bunch of data.

01:09:24.782 --> 01:09:26.330
The obvious thing
to do is I'd say,

01:09:26.330 --> 01:09:27.750
could I please
have all your data?

01:09:27.750 --> 01:09:31.189
So I would collect all
your data and then fit

01:09:31.189 --> 01:09:33.620
a model in distributed.

01:09:33.620 --> 01:09:36.300
The way it works in distributed
convex objects is very cool.

01:09:36.300 --> 01:09:39.064
It works like this.

01:09:39.064 --> 01:09:40.939
I don't get your data,
I don't get your data,

01:09:40.939 --> 01:09:44.960
I don't get your data,
but I coordinate you.

01:09:44.960 --> 01:09:48.890
So I'd say you fit a logistic
model on your own data.

01:09:48.890 --> 01:09:50.580
Everybody got that?

01:09:50.580 --> 01:09:54.350
Then all you do is you
send your models to me.

01:09:54.350 --> 01:09:56.870
I do a very small
amount of computation.

01:09:56.870 --> 01:09:59.030
And I send a-- oops,
sorry about that,

01:09:59.030 --> 01:10:02.850
and I send a
message back to you.

01:10:02.850 --> 01:10:07.030
You plug another term
into your models and refit

01:10:07.030 --> 01:10:09.880
and this goes 10 steps
order of magnitude.

01:10:09.880 --> 01:10:14.620
And at that point, we've
converged on the same model.

01:10:14.620 --> 01:10:17.080
You actually have no idea
how many other hospitals

01:10:17.080 --> 01:10:18.790
I'm talking to.

01:10:18.790 --> 01:10:19.870
But guess what?

01:10:19.870 --> 01:10:23.680
We just together
solved this problem

01:10:23.680 --> 01:10:26.470
that's identical to had I
collected all of your data

01:10:26.470 --> 01:10:28.300
in one place and
done it all once.

01:10:28.300 --> 01:10:30.160
Anyway, everyone get that?

01:10:30.160 --> 01:10:31.000
So that's in 364B.

01:10:31.000 --> 01:10:32.750
It's actually in a
couple of other things,

01:10:32.750 --> 01:10:34.150
but these are
pretty cool things.

01:10:34.150 --> 01:10:36.010
We didn't talk about
it at all here.

01:10:36.010 --> 01:10:38.950
Not surprisingly it uses
ideas like duality and stuff

01:10:38.950 --> 01:10:41.200
like that.

01:10:41.200 --> 01:10:44.590
And the other one is 392F.

01:10:44.590 --> 01:10:45.490
Is that right?

01:10:45.490 --> 01:10:50.620
So that's taught by Ernest Ryu.

01:10:50.620 --> 01:10:52.960
He's a professor at Seoul
National University.

01:10:52.960 --> 01:10:57.940
He just wrote a beautiful book
on monotone operator methods.

01:10:57.940 --> 01:11:00.633
It'll be a little more
mathematical than this one.

01:11:00.633 --> 01:11:02.050
But it's going to
be a weird thing

01:11:02.050 --> 01:11:04.600
because it will look like math.

01:11:04.600 --> 01:11:09.320
But in fact, everything
in it is it's

01:11:09.320 --> 01:11:12.470
very close to extremely
useful things.

01:11:12.470 --> 01:11:15.470
If you took that
course, actually

01:11:15.470 --> 01:11:17.360
stayed awake through the math.

01:11:17.360 --> 01:11:19.970
You're just one Torch
implementation away

01:11:19.970 --> 01:11:24.380
from actually an extremely,
good very large-scale solver

01:11:24.380 --> 01:11:25.040
for something.

01:11:25.040 --> 01:11:26.330
Everybody got that?

01:11:26.330 --> 01:11:27.980
So that's a cool thing.

01:11:27.980 --> 01:11:29.900
So I think-- let's see.

01:11:29.900 --> 01:11:32.300
What I'll do now is--

01:11:32.300 --> 01:11:36.380
oh, so I have to thank the TAs.

01:11:36.380 --> 01:11:38.360
You probably-- I
don't know, if you

01:11:38.360 --> 01:11:41.570
have any idea [LAUGHS] how much
is going on behind the scenes.

01:11:41.570 --> 01:11:44.210
A lot of it is chaotic and
you wouldn't want to know.

01:11:44.210 --> 01:11:48.170
But mostly, it's just
a whole lot of work

01:11:48.170 --> 01:11:51.440
just making sure
scripts actually still

01:11:51.440 --> 01:11:54.690
work with updates in CVXPY
and all sorts of other stuff.

01:11:54.690 --> 01:12:01.790
So, anyway, it'd be impossible
without tons of help from them.

01:12:01.790 --> 01:12:04.310
And I think we're done.

01:12:04.310 --> 01:12:08.780
You've mostly survived, I think.

01:12:08.780 --> 01:12:09.280
Yes.

01:12:09.280 --> 01:12:11.210
Do you want to mention A-222?

01:12:11.210 --> 01:12:11.710
Oh, sorry.

01:12:11.710 --> 01:12:13.430
I should have mentioned
Michael's class.

01:12:13.430 --> 01:12:13.930
OK.

01:12:13.930 --> 01:12:19.060
So other things I would point
you to that'd be very cool

01:12:19.060 --> 01:12:22.180
is actually, I just point you
to all of Mykel Kochenderfer

01:12:22.180 --> 01:12:25.400
classes, all of them, and books.

01:12:25.400 --> 01:12:26.920
So it's simple.

01:12:26.920 --> 01:12:30.352
Just read them, take the
classes, whatever you want.

01:12:30.352 --> 01:12:31.810
They're going to
be extremely good.

01:12:31.810 --> 01:12:33.580
They're quite different.

01:12:33.580 --> 01:12:38.890
So they're more-- we focused
on one idea like convexity

01:12:38.890 --> 01:12:42.250
and for that matter very
small number of methods,

01:12:42.250 --> 01:12:45.072
his courses are more like
we're going to do optimization.

01:12:45.072 --> 01:12:46.780
We don't care if it's
convex, non-convex,

01:12:46.780 --> 01:12:48.988
and we're going to look at
multiple methods to do it.

01:12:48.988 --> 01:12:52.960
But just beautiful, clear
book, incredibly good classes.

01:12:52.960 --> 01:12:56.805
So I would actually strongly
urge you to look at those.

01:12:56.805 --> 01:12:58.180
Some people don't
know about them

01:12:58.180 --> 01:13:00.910
because I guess they're
listed in AeroAstro,

01:13:00.910 --> 01:13:02.440
and then maybe
they're cross-listed

01:13:02.440 --> 01:13:03.400
in CS or something.

01:13:03.400 --> 01:13:06.100
So people in CS know about it,
but I think many others don't.

01:13:06.100 --> 01:13:10.550
So I would strongly
recommend those.

